ForgeData.register("bio",{"BIO-1":{"label":"Cell Biology","color":"#15803d","questions":[{"id":"BIO-01","stem":"A student says mitochondria produce ATP by 'burning glucose.' Which term more accurately describes this process?","options":{"A":"Aerobic respiration — glucose is oxidised via glycolysis, the Krebs cycle","B":"Fermentation — glucose is broken down without oxygen.","C":"Stroma, where the enzyme rubisco fixes carbon dioxide into glycerate 3-phosphate.","D":"Hydrolysis — water breaks the bonds in glucose to release energy."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-RESPIRATION-PHOTOSYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Aerobic respiration: glucose + oxygen → CO₂ + water + ATP (up to ~38 ATP per glucose). Three stages: glycolysis (cytoplasm, produces 2 ATP + pyruvate), link reaction and Krebs cycle (mitochondrial matrix), oxidative phosphorylation (inner mitochondrial membrane, uses electron transport chain). 'Burning' implies combustion — respiration is an enzyme-controlled series of redox reactions.","reforge":{"stem":"Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm and produces a net gain of 2 ATP per glucose. Where does the pyruvate produced go next in aerobic respiration?","options":{"A":"It is excreted as a waste product.","B":"It is converted back to glucose in the liver.","C":"It enters the mitochondrial matrix for the link reaction, where it is converted to acetyl-CoA, CO₂, and reduced NAD.","D":"Muscle temperature always falls during exercise in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.7"},{"id":"BIO-02","stem":"Which correctly distinguishes prokaryotic from eukaryotic cells?","options":{"A":"A rigid bilayer of proteins with phospholipids floating through channels.","B":"Prokaryotes lack a membrane-bound nucleus and membrane-bound organelles","C":"Prokaryotes are always larger than eukaryotes.","D":"Eukaryotes cannot reproduce asexually."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Prokaryotes (bacteria, archaea): no nucleus (naked DNA, often circular, in nucleoid region), no membrane-bound organelles, smaller (1-10μm), 70S ribosomes, may have plasmids, flagella, pili. Eukaryotes (animals, plants, fungi, protists): nucleus with nuclear envelope, membrane-bound organelles (mitochondria, ER, Golgi), larger (10-100μm), 80S ribosomes (70S in mitochondria/chloroplasts — evidence for endosymbiosis).","reforge":{"stem":"Streptomycin is an antibiotic that binds to 70S ribosomes and disrupts protein synthesis. Why does it selectively kill bacteria without harming human cells?","options":{"A":"Human cells do not produce proteins, so ribosomes are unnecessary.","B":"The antibiotic is too large to enter eukaryotic cells.","C":"Bacteria lack a cell wall so the antibiotic enters more easily.","D":"Human cells have 80S ribosomes, which streptomycin cannot bind"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-03","stem":"During DNA replication, which enzyme unwinds the double helix by breaking hydrogen bonds between base pairs?","options":{"A":"DNA polymerase","B":"Ligase","C":"Helicase","D":"Primase"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"Replication enzymes: Helicase — unwinds/unzips the double helix (breaks H-bonds). Primase — synthesises RNA primer to provide a starting point. DNA polymerase III — adds nucleotides in 5'→3' direction (needs a primer, cannot start from scratch). DNA polymerase I — removes RNA primer, replaces with DNA. Ligase — joins Okazaki fragments on lagging strand. Students frequently confuse helicase with DNA polymerase.","reforge":{"stem":"DNA replication is described as semi-conservative. This means:","options":{"A":"Each new molecule keeps one parent strand and one new strand","B":"Only half the DNA is replicated in each cell cycle.","C":"Half the base pairs are conserved unchanged; the other half are replaced.","D":"Replication only occurs during the S phase for some chromosomes, not all."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.3"},{"id":"BIO-04","stem":"Facilitated diffusion differs from simple diffusion in that it:","options":{"A":"Facilitated diffusion requires ATP to open channel proteins; active transport is entirely passive.","B":"Only moves water molecules across the phospholipid bilayer.","C":"Proceeds faster when the concentration gradient is smaller.","D":"Uses protein channels or carriers to move polar or large molecules down their concentratio"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Simple diffusion: small, non-polar molecules (O₂, CO₂, steroids) pass directly through the phospholipid bilayer, down their concentration gradient, no ATP. Facilitated diffusion: ions, glucose, amino acids — too large or polar to cross directly — use channel proteins (ions) or carrier proteins (glucose) — still passive (down gradient), still no ATP. Active transport: uses ATP + carrier proteins, AGAINST gradient.","reforge":{"stem":"Glucose absorption in the small intestine uses both facilitated diffusion and active transport. Active transport is needed because:","options":{"A":"Glucose molecules are too large for protein channels.","B":"Glucose must move into epithelial cells against its gradient, needing ATP","C":"Facilitated diffusion is too slow for the rate of glucose absorption needed after a meal.","D":"Active transport allows glucose to move in both directions simultaneously."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.5"},{"id":"BIO-05","stem":"A student calculates magnification using: actual size = image size × magnification. Why is this formula wrong?","options":{"A":"The correct formula is: magnification = image size ÷ actual size","B":"that is the correct formula.","C":"Magnification cannot be calculated from size measurements alone.","D":"The formula is correct but only applies to electron microscopes."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"MAG formula triangle: Magnification = Image size ÷ Actual size. Image size = Magnification × Actual size. Actual size = Image size ÷ Magnification. Always ensure units match. Example: image = 50mm, actual = 0.005mm → M = 50 ÷ 0.005 = ×10,000. Common error: multiplying instead of dividing, or mixing mm and μm. Convert everything to the same unit before calculating.","reforge":{"stem":"A cell appears 30mm wide in a micrograph taken at ×15,000 magnification. What is the actual width of the cell in μm?","options":{"A":"Uses ATP to move molecules against a concentration gradient.","B":"0.002 μm","C":"2 μm (30mm = 30,000 μm; actual = 30,000 ÷ 15,000 = 2 μm)","D":"30 μm"},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-06","stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the fluid mosaic model of the cell membrane?","options":{"A":"A rigid bilayer of proteins with phospholipids floating through channels.","B":"A phospholipid bilayer in which proteins are embedded or attached, with both lipids and proteins able to move laterally","C":"A fixed arrangement of cholesterol molecules with protein channels at regular intervals.","D":"Muscle temperature always falls during exercise in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Fluid mosaic model (Singer and Nicolson, 1972): phospholipid bilayer is fluid (lateral movement of lipids and proteins). Mosaic = varied protein types scattered throughout. Cholesterol = regulates fluidity (less fluid at high temps, more fluid at low temps). Proteins: integral (transmembrane — channels, carriers), peripheral (surface — structural, enzymes). Glycoproteins and glycolipids = cell recognition.","reforge":{"stem":"Cholesterol is an important component of animal cell membranes because it:","options":{"A":"Provides energy when the cell needs to actively transport molecules.","B":"Provides the hydrophobic core that prevents all large molecules from crossing.","C":"Forms the receptor sites for all membrane-bound enzymes.","D":"Stabilises membrane fluidity across a range of temperatures"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.5"},{"id":"BIO-07","stem":"In a mitosis diagram, a student labels a cell with 4 chromosomes as being in 'S phase.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"S phase cells have 4 chromosomes.","B":"S phase cells always have 2 chromosomes, not 4.","C":"S phase is part of interphase (not mitosis)","D":"S phase occurs after mitosis is complete."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-DIVISION","scaffold":"Cell cycle: Interphase (G1, S, G2) → Mitosis (prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase) → Cytokinesis. S phase = DNA synthesis/replication — chromosomes not condensed, not visible under light microscope. Mitosis stages: Prophase (chromosomes condense, spindle forms), Metaphase (chromosomes align at equator), Anaphase (chromatids separate to poles), Telophase (nuclear envelopes reform). Cytokinesis splits the cytoplasm.","reforge":{"stem":"During which stage of mitosis do sister chromatids separate and move to opposite poles?","options":{"A":"Anaphase — spindle fibres shorten, pulling sister chromatids to opposite poles of the cell.","B":"Metaphase — chromosomes are aligned at the equator.","C":"Yes — crossing over moves one segment while independent assortment randomises all chromosomes.","D":"Prophase — chromosomes condense and become visible."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.6"},{"id":"BIO-08","stem":"An investigation tests the effect of temperature on enzyme activity. A student says: 'The enzyme is destroyed above 60°C.' Which more precise term should be used?","options":{"A":"The rate increases then plateaus immediately — doubling enzyme has no effect when substrate is in excess.","B":"Oxidised — the enzyme loses electrons at high temperature, altering its charge.","C":"Hydrolysed — water molecules break peptide bonds within the enzyme structure.","D":"Denatured — the high temperature disrupts hydrogen bonds, changing the active site shape permanently."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Denaturation = permanent change to tertiary/quaternary structure. At high temperatures: increased kinetic energy → hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds, and some disulfide bonds break → tertiary structure changes → active site changes shape → substrate no longer complementary → no enzyme-substrate complexes form → reaction stops. This is irreversible. Contrast with inhibition (reversible, competitive or non-competitive) where structure may be unchanged.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do enzymes have an optimum pH as well as an optimum temperature?","options":{"A":"pH affects the solubility of the substrate rather than the enzyme.","B":"pH alters R-group ionisation, disrupting bonds that hold the active site","C":"Enzymes can only function in neutral conditions (pH 7).","D":"pH affects the speed of product release rather than the rate of substrate binding."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-E1-01","spec":"BIO-1","stem":"A cell surface membrane is described as partially permeable because it:","options":{"A":"allows some substances through more readily than others","B":"Human cells do not produce proteins, so ribosomes are unnecessary.","C":"contains no proteins","D":"prevents water movement"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Phospholipids and membrane proteins create selective movement into and out of cells.","reforge":{"stem":"Glucose molecules cross a cell membrane far more easily than large protein molecules do. What property of the membrane does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Partial permeability, letting some substances through more readily than others","B":"A membrane that allows every solute through equally in a typical cell in this biological system","C":"A membrane that contains no proteins at all","D":"A membrane that prevents water movement entirely"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.5"},{"id":"BIO-E1-02","spec":"BIO-1","stem":"The function of a mitochondrion is most directly linked to:","options":{"A":"Fermentation — glucose is broken down without oxygen.","B":"aerobic respiration and ATP production","C":"cellulose synthesis","D":"DNA storage only"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-RESPIRATION-PHOTOSYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Mitochondria contain sites of aerobic respiration and ATP-generating reactions.","reforge":{"stem":"Muscle cells that need large amounts of energy contain unusually high numbers of a particular organelle. Which organelle, and why?","options":{"A":"The organelle responsible only for protein secretion in a typical cell","B":"The organelle responsible for cellulose synthesis","C":"The mitochondrion, the site of aerobic respiration and ATP production","D":"The organelle responsible only for DNA storage"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-E1-03","spec":"BIO-1","stem":"A peptide bond joins:","options":{"A":"fatty acids only","B":"monosaccharides only","C":"amino acids","D":"phosphate ions"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"Peptide bonds form between amino acids during condensation reactions.","reforge":{"stem":"During protein synthesis, the carboxyl group of one monomer reacts with the amino group of the next, releasing water. What bond forms, and between which monomers?","options":{"A":"A bond joining fatty acids only","B":"A bond joining monosaccharides only in a typical cell","C":"A bond joining phosphate ions","D":"A peptide bond, joining amino acids together"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.2"},{"id":"BIO-E1-04","spec":"BIO-1","stem":"DNA replication is described as semi-conservative because:","options":{"A":"both strands are entirely new","B":"only RNA is copied","C":"Half the base pairs are conserved unchanged; the other half are replaced.","D":"each new molecule contains one original strand and one new strand"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"Base pairing lets each original strand act as a template for a new complementary strand.","reforge":{"stem":"After one round of DNA replication, each new double helix is found to contain one original strand and one newly synthesised strand. What does this confirm about the replication mechanism?","options":{"A":"It is semi-conservative, since each molecule keeps one original strand","B":"Both strands produced are entirely new","C":"Only RNA is copied during the process","D":"DNA is replicated only inside mitochondria in a typical cell in this biological system"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.3"},{"id":"BIO-E1B-01","spec":"BIO-1","stem":"The role of ribosomes is to:","options":{"A":"assemble polypeptides during translation","B":"Only half the DNA is replicated in each cell cycle.","C":"produce ATP by glycolysis","D":"store lipids"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"Ribosomes translate mRNA codons into a polypeptide sequence.","reforge":{"stem":"A cell organelle reads the sequence on a strand of messenger RNA and links amino acids together in the corresponding order. What is this organelle, and what is it doing?","options":{"A":"An organelle replicating whole chromosomes in a typical cell","B":"A ribosome, assembling polypeptides during translation","C":"An organelle producing ATP by glycolysis","D":"An organelle storing lipids"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-GAP1-01","spec":"BIO-1","stem":"In an investigation, reliability is improved most directly by:","options":{"A":"changing several variables together","B":"repeating measurements and comparing the results","C":"Because using fewer decimal places reduces the uncertainty in a typical cell in this biological system","D":"using a smaller sample"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"Repeats help reveal random variation and make a mean more dependable.","reforge":{"stem":"A student repeats an experiment three times and finds the results are very similar each time. Why does this most directly support the reliability of the investigation?","options":{"A":"Changing several variables together produces a clearer result","B":"Removing all anomalous data without checking improves the outcome in a typical cell","C":"Using a smaller sample size makes the result more trustworthy","D":"Repeating measurements and comparing the results shows the findings are consistent"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-1.1"},{"id":"BIO-GAP1-02","spec":"BIO-1","stem":"A student measures the effect of light intensity on photosynthesis. Which is a suitable control variable?","options":{"A":"light intensity being changed","B":"the measured oxygen volume","C":"temperature of the water bath","D":"the final conclusion"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"Temperature can affect enzyme activity, so it should be kept constant.","reforge":{"stem":"A student investigates how light intensity affects the rate of photosynthesis in pondweed, measuring the volume of oxygen produced. Which variable should be kept constant throughout the investigation?","options":{"A":"The temperature of the water bath","B":"The light intensity being changed","C":"The measured oxygen volume","D":"The final conclusion drawn from the results"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.6"},{"id":"BIO-GAP1-03","spec":"BIO-1","stem":"A low percentage uncertainty is generally obtained by:","options":{"A":"using fewer decimal places","B":"ignoring the measuring instrument","C":"Removing all anomalous data without checking improves the outcome in a typical cell","D":"measuring a larger quantity with the same absolute uncertainty"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"Percentage uncertainty is absolute uncertainty divided by the measured value.","reforge":{"stem":"A student measures a large volume of liquid using a measuring cylinder with a fixed absolute uncertainty. Why does measuring a larger quantity typically give a lower percentage uncertainty?","options":{"A":"Because using fewer decimal places reduces the uncertainty in a typical cell in this biological system","B":"Because the same absolute uncertainty becomes a smaller fraction of a larger measured value","C":"Because the measuring instrument itself can be ignored","D":"Because the independent variable changes during the trial"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-1.1"},{"id":"BIO-GAP1-04","spec":"BIO-1","stem":"The cardiac cycle moves blood because:","options":{"A":"pressure differences are produced by coordinated contraction and relaxation","B":"The pulmonary artery, carrying oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart in a typical cell","C":"valves create oxygen","D":"all chambers contract permanently"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Muscle contraction changes pressure and valves help maintain one-way flow.","reforge":{"stem":"Blood is pushed through the heart's chambers and out into the arteries without the arteries themselves contracting. What actually generates the pressure differences that move the blood?","options":{"A":"Arteries actively pumping the blood themselves in a typical cell","B":"Valves creating oxygen as blood passes through","C":"Coordinated contraction and relaxation of the heart's chambers","D":"All chambers contracting permanently at once"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-N1-01","stem":"A student increases the magnification of a light microscope but the two structures still appear as one blur. This is because the microscope is limited by its:","options":{"A":"Magnification, which cannot exceed ×400 on any instrument.","B":"Resolution — how far apart two points must be to be seen separately","C":"Eyepiece graticule, which must be recalibrated for every objective lens used.","D":"Depth of field, which determines how much of the specimen is in focus at once."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Magnification = how many times larger the image is. Resolution = the ability to distinguish two close points as separate, set by the wavelength of the radiation used. Light (~400-700nm) gives ~200nm resolution; electrons (much shorter wavelength) give ~0.1nm. Magnifying beyond the resolving limit gives 'empty magnification' — a bigger but no clearer image. This is why organelles like ribosomes need an electron microscope.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a transmission electron microscope resolve ribosomes when a light microscope cannot?","options":{"A":"Electron microscopes use a much higher magnification setting.","B":"Light microscopes cannot be used on dead tissue, and ribosomes break down in living cells.","C":"Ribosomes are stained more darkly in electron microscopy preparations.","D":"Electrons have a far shorter wavelength than light, so the resolving limit is much smaller"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-02","stem":"Which option correctly compares a transmission electron microscope (TEM) with a scanning electron microscope (SEM)?","options":{"A":"TEM produces a 3D surface view; SEM produces a 2D section.","B":"Both can be used on living specimens held in a vacuum chamber.","C":"TEM transmits electrons through a section; SEM reflects them","D":"SEM has a higher resolution than TEM in all standard applications."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"TEM: electrons transmitted through an ultra-thin section, denser regions absorb more electrons and appear darker, gives a 2D internal image, highest resolution (~0.1nm). SEM: electrons bounced off a surface coated in metal, gives a 3D surface image, lower resolution (~5-10nm). Both require a vacuum, so specimens must be dead — a common exam trap. Both need staining with heavy metals, which can create artefacts.","reforge":{"stem":"A researcher wants a three-dimensional image of the surface of a pollen grain. Which instrument and reason are correct?","options":{"A":"SEM, because reflected electrons build a 3D surface image","B":"Light microscope, because the specimen can remain alive throughout.","C":"TEM, because electrons pass through the specimen to build depth.","D":"TEM, because it has the highest available resolution."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-03","stem":"The Golgi apparatus is best described as the organelle that:","options":{"A":"Synthesises ATP using an electron transport chain across folded membranes.","B":"Assembles polypeptides by reading the codon sequence carried on messenger RNA.","C":"Contains hydrolytic enzymes that digest worn-out organelles and engulfed material.","D":"Modifies, sorts and packages proteins into vesicles for secretion"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Secretory pathway order: ribosome on RER makes polypeptide → RER folds it → transport vesicle → Golgi (cis face receives, trans face releases) modifies it (adds carbohydrate to form glycoproteins, trims chains) → secretory vesicle → cell surface membrane (exocytosis). The Golgi also forms lysosomes. Students often say the Golgi 'makes' proteins — it modifies and packages them; the ribosome makes them.","reforge":{"stem":"Goblet cells secreting mucus have an unusually large Golgi apparatus. What does this most directly indicate?","options":{"A":"A high rate of aerobic respiration to supply ATP.","B":"A high rate of packaging glycoproteins for export","C":"A large store of hydrolytic enzymes for digesting bacteria.","D":"Extensive DNA replication in preparation for cell division."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-04","stem":"Rough endoplasmic reticulum differs from smooth endoplasmic reticulum because only rough ER:","options":{"A":"Has ribosomes bound to its outer surface, so it processes polypeptides","B":"Synthesises and stores lipids, phospholipids and steroid hormones.","C":"Is found exclusively in animal cells rather than in plant cells.","D":"Is enclosed by a double membrane continuous with the nuclear envelope."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"RER: ribosomes attached to the cytosolic surface, folds and transports proteins destined for secretion or membranes — abundant in pancreatic and plasma cells. SER: no ribosomes, makes lipids and steroids, stores and releases calcium ions — abundant in liver cells and muscle (sarcoplasmic reticulum). Both are continuous with the nuclear envelope. The presence or absence of ribosomes is the defining difference.","reforge":{"stem":"Liver cells that detoxify drugs contain extensive smooth endoplasmic reticulum. This is because SER:","options":{"A":"Provides attachment sites for ribosomes making detoxifying enzymes.","B":"Stores the cell's genetic information for enzyme synthesis.","C":"Carries enzymes that metabolise lipid-soluble drugs","D":"Generates the ATP required for active transport of toxins."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-05","stem":"Lysosomes are able to digest cellular material without destroying the rest of the cell because:","options":{"A":"Their enzymes are permanently inactive until the cell dies.","B":"Their hydrolytic enzymes are enclosed within a single membrane","C":"They contain no enzymes, only concentrated acid for chemical breakdown.","D":"They are located only in the nucleus, away from the cytoplasmic organelles."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Lysosomes are membrane-bound vesicles from the Golgi containing hydrolases (proteases, lipases, nucleases) that work at an acidic pH (~5), maintained by proton pumps in the lysosomal membrane. Roles: digesting material taken in by phagocytosis, autophagy of worn organelles, apoptosis, and releasing enzymes outside the cell (e.g. the acrosome in sperm). The cytosol is near pH 7, so leaked enzymes work poorly — a second layer of protection.","reforge":{"stem":"The acrosome of a sperm cell is a specialised lysosome. Its function is to:","options":{"A":"Supply ATP for the movement of the flagellum.","B":"Recycle worn-out mitochondria in the sperm midpiece.","C":"Store the haploid nucleus until fertilisation occurs.","D":"Release enzymes that digest a path to the egg"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-06","stem":"Mitochondria and chloroplasts both contain 70S ribosomes and circular DNA. This observation is used as evidence for:","options":{"A":"The fluid mosaic model of membrane structure.","B":"The semi-conservative nature of DNA replication.","C":"The endosymbiotic theory of eukaryotic organelle origin","D":"The principle that all cells arise from pre-existing cells."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Endosymbiotic theory: mitochondria and chloroplasts descend from free-living prokaryotes engulfed by an ancestral eukaryote. Evidence: 70S (prokaryote-type) ribosomes, circular naked DNA, double membrane (inner from the prokaryote, outer from the host vesicle), self-replication by binary fission, and their own protein synthesis. This also explains why some antibiotics targeting 70S ribosomes have mitochondrial side effects.","reforge":{"stem":"Which additional feature of mitochondria supports the endosymbiotic theory?","options":{"A":"They divide by binary fission independently of nuclear division","B":"Their enzymes are synthesised entirely by ribosomes in the cytosol.","C":"They are found only in animal cells and never in plants.","D":"They are surrounded by a single phospholipid membrane."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-07","stem":"Plant cell walls and fungal cell walls differ in that plant walls are made mainly of:","options":{"A":"Peptidoglycan, whereas fungal walls are made of cellulose.","B":"Glycogen, whereas fungal walls are built from cross-linked structural proteins.","C":"Chitin, whereas fungal walls are made largely of cellulose.","D":"Cellulose, whereas fungal walls are made largely of chitin"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Cell wall composition by group: plants — cellulose (β-glucose, straight chains, hydrogen-bonded into microfibrils); fungi — chitin (a nitrogen-containing polysaccharide); bacteria — peptidoglycan (murein, a polysaccharide cross-linked by peptides, the target of penicillin). All resist osmotic lysis by exerting an inward pressure. Confusing chitin with cellulose is one of the most common classification errors.","reforge":{"stem":"Penicillin inhibits the cross-linking of peptidoglycan. Why does this harm bacteria but not human cells?","options":{"A":"Human cells have peptidoglycan only in the nuclear envelope.","B":"Human cells have no cell wall and no peptidoglycan","C":"Human cells replace their cell walls rapidly enough to compensate.","D":"Human cells use chitin rather than peptidoglycan in their walls."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-08","stem":"In a chloroplast, the light-dependent reactions occur on the:","options":{"A":"Thylakoid membranes, which hold the photosystems and electron carriers","B":"Outer chloroplast membrane, which is freely permeable to small molecules.","C":"Intergranal lamellae only, which contain no photosynthetic pigment.","D":"Stroma, where the enzyme rubisco fixes carbon dioxide into glycerate 3-phosphate."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-RESPIRATION-PHOTOSYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Chloroplast structure maps onto function. Thylakoid membranes (stacked into grana) carry chlorophyll in photosystems I and II, electron carriers and ATP synthase — site of the light-dependent stage producing ATP, reduced NADP and oxygen. The stroma is the fluid matrix containing rubisco, and is the site of the light-independent stage (Calvin cycle). A large surface area of thylakoid maximises light capture.","reforge":{"stem":"Which product of the light-dependent reactions is required by the Calvin cycle in the stroma?","options":{"A":"Oxygen released from the photolysis of water.","B":"Carbon dioxide diffusing in through the stomata.","C":"Reduced NADP, used to reduce GP to triose phosphate","D":"Chlorophyll transported out of the thylakoid membranes."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-09","stem":"The nucleolus is the region of the nucleus responsible for:","options":{"A":"Controlling the movement of mRNA through nuclear pores.","B":"Manufacturing ribosomal RNA and assembling ribosome subunits","C":"Storing the cell's supply of ATP for nuclear division.","D":"Separating sister chromatids during the anaphase stage of mitosis."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"The nucleolus is a dense, non-membrane-bound region of the nucleus where rRNA is transcribed and combined with protein to form ribosome subunits, which then leave through nuclear pores. Cells with high protein synthesis rates (e.g. secretory cells) have prominent nucleoli. Distinguish it from: the nuclear envelope (double membrane with pores), chromatin (DNA + histones), and the nuclear pores themselves (control mRNA export).","reforge":{"stem":"A cell is observed to have a very large nucleolus. What is the most reasonable inference?","options":{"A":"The cell is about to undergo meiosis rather than mitosis.","B":"The cell contains an unusually large number of lysosomes.","C":"The cell has stopped transcribing DNA entirely.","D":"The cell is making ribosomes rapidly for protein output"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-10","stem":"Water's ability to act as a transport medium in xylem depends most directly on:","options":{"A":"Its low specific heat capacity, which allows rapid temperature change.","B":"Its role as a non-polar solvent for lipids and steroid hormones.","C":"Hydrogen bonding, which produces cohesion between water molecules","D":"Its high density at 4°C, which causes ice to float on liquid water."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Water is polar (uneven charge distribution) so molecules hydrogen-bond to each other. Consequences: cohesion (columns of water are pulled up xylem without breaking), adhesion to vessel walls, high surface tension, high specific heat capacity (stable temperatures), high latent heat of vaporisation (evaporative cooling), and excellent solvent properties for polar and ionic solutes. Note water is a poor solvent for non-polar substances such as lipids.","reforge":{"stem":"The cohesion-tension theory explains water movement up the xylem. Cohesion refers to:","options":{"A":"Hydrogen bonds holding water molecules in a continuous column","B":"The evaporation of water from the spongy mesophyll into air spaces.","C":"The active transport of water molecules by carrier proteins.","D":"The attraction between water molecules and the lignified vessel wall."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.3"},{"id":"BIO-N1-11","stem":"A condensation reaction between two monosaccharides produces:","options":{"A":"Two smaller monomers and one molecule of ATP.","B":"A polypeptide chain linked by peptide bonds between amino groups.","C":"Two monosaccharides with an added hydroxyl group each.","D":"A disaccharide and a molecule of water"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Condensation: monomers join, a covalent bond forms, and one water molecule is released per bond. Hydrolysis is the reverse — water is used to break the bond. Bond names by molecule: glycosidic (carbohydrates), peptide (proteins), ester (triglycerides), phosphodiester (nucleic acids). Counting trick: joining n monomers into a chain releases n−1 water molecules.","reforge":{"stem":"How many water molecules are released when 8 amino acids join to form a single polypeptide?","options":{"A":"8, one for each amino acid in the chain.","B":"7, because one water is lost per peptide bond formed","C":"16, two for each peptide bond in the chain.","D":"0, because peptide bond formation is a hydrolysis reaction."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-12","stem":"Cellulose is suited to its structural role because it consists of:","options":{"A":"Straight β-glucose chains hydrogen-bonded into strong microfibrils","B":"Coiled α-glucose chains held by 1,4 and 1,6 glycosidic bonds.","C":"Amino acids joined by disulfide bridges into a fibrous sheet.","D":"Branched chains of α-glucose that pack into dense compact granules."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"α-glucose polymers are storage molecules: starch (amylose — unbranched helix; amylopectin — branched, 1,6 bonds give many ends for rapid hydrolysis) in plants, glycogen (more branched still) in animals. β-glucose polymers are structural: alternate monomers flip 180°, giving straight chains that hydrogen-bond side by side into microfibrils of great tensile strength. The α/β distinction is the whole explanation for the difference in function.","reforge":{"stem":"Glycogen is more highly branched than amylose. What advantage does this give animals?","options":{"A":"It makes glycogen soluble enough to be transported in the blood.","B":"It allows glycogen to be used as a structural component of cell walls.","C":"More free ends allow faster hydrolysis to glucose when demand rises","D":"It prevents glycogen from ever being broken down by enzymes."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-13","stem":"A phospholipid differs from a triglyceride in that a phospholipid has:","options":{"A":"Three fatty acids attached to a glycerol backbone by ester bonds.","B":"One fatty acid replaced by a phosphate, making it amphipathic","C":"No glycerol component in its molecular structure at all.","D":"A hydrophilic tail region and a hydrophobic head group."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Triglyceride = glycerol + 3 fatty acids (3 ester bonds), entirely hydrophobic, used for energy storage, insulation and protection. Phospholipid = glycerol + 2 fatty acids + phosphate group. The phosphate head is hydrophilic (polar), the fatty acid tails hydrophobic. In water, phospholipids self-assemble into a bilayer with tails inward — the structural basis of all cell membranes. Note option D reverses head and tail: the head is hydrophilic.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do phospholipids spontaneously form a bilayer when mixed with water?","options":{"A":"All parts of the molecule are repelled by water equally.","B":"Phosphate groups form covalent bonds with surrounding water molecules.","C":"The ester bonds are hydrolysed, releasing the fatty acid tails.","D":"Hydrophilic heads face the water while hydrophobic tails face in"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.2"},{"id":"BIO-N1-14","stem":"The tertiary structure of a protein is best described as:","options":{"A":"The sequence of amino acids held together by peptide bonds.","B":"The α-helix or β-pleated sheet formed by hydrogen bonding.","C":"The overall 3D shape of one polypeptide, held by R-group interactions","D":"The association of two or more polypeptide chains into one functional protein."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"Primary — amino acid sequence (peptide bonds). Secondary — local folding into α-helix or β-pleated sheet (hydrogen bonds between backbone C=O and N-H). Tertiary — the whole chain folds into a precise 3D shape, held by hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds between charged R-groups, disulfide bridges between cysteines, and hydrophobic interactions. Quaternary — two or more polypeptides (e.g. haemoglobin's four chains), sometimes with a prosthetic group.","reforge":{"stem":"Haemoglobin consists of four polypeptide chains each bound to a haem group. Which structural level does this describe?","options":{"A":"Quaternary structure, because several polypeptides form one protein","B":"Secondary structure, because α-helices are present in each chain.","C":"Tertiary structure, because each chain folds into a 3D shape.","D":"Primary structure, because the amino acid sequence determines everything."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.2"},{"id":"BIO-N1-15","stem":"A mutation replaces a cysteine residue in the interior of an enzyme with alanine. The most likely direct consequence is:","options":{"A":"The primary structure is unaffected by the substitution.","B":"Transcription of the gene stops completely at that point.","C":"The enzyme gains an additional active site elsewhere on the chain.","D":"A disulfide bridge cannot form, so tertiary structure destabilises"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"Disulfide bridges are strong covalent bonds between the -SH groups of two cysteine residues. They stabilise tertiary structure, and are common in extracellular proteins (e.g. antibodies, keratin) that must resist harsh conditions. Losing one usually makes the protein less stable and more easily denatured; if it distorts the active site the enzyme loses activity. Note the primary structure IS changed — the amino acid sequence differs.","reforge":{"stem":"Keratin in hair is rich in cysteine. Why does a chemical that reduces disulfide bridges allow hair to be reshaped?","options":{"A":"It converts keratin from a globular protein into a fibrous one.","B":"Breaking those cross-links lets the chains slide and re-set","C":"It hydrolyses every peptide bond in the keratin chains.","D":"It replaces cysteine residues with alanine along the chain."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N1-16","stem":"Globular proteins such as enzymes differ from fibrous proteins such as collagen because globular proteins are typically:","options":{"A":"Soluble and compact, with hydrophilic R-groups outermost","B":"Insoluble, with a highly repetitive amino acid sequence.","C":"Unable to be denatured by changes in temperature or pH.","D":"Composed only of non-polar amino acids throughout the chain."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"Globular: compact spherical tertiary structure, hydrophobic R-groups turned inward and hydrophilic outward, therefore soluble; roles are metabolic (enzymes, haemoglobin, antibodies, insulin); sequence is irregular and precise. Fibrous: long parallel chains, often cross-linked, repetitive sequence, insoluble, structural roles (collagen, keratin, elastin). Solubility follows directly from where the hydrophilic groups sit.","reforge":{"stem":"Collagen's strength comes largely from three polypeptide chains wound together and cross-linked. This makes collagen suitable for:","options":{"A":"Catalysing reactions in the cytoplasm at high rates.","B":"Transporting oxygen around the body in red blood cells.","C":"Structural roles such as tendons and artery walls","D":"Acting as a soluble hormone carried in the bloodstream."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N1-17","stem":"A student heats a food sample with Benedict's reagent and obtains a brick-red precipitate. This shows the presence of:","options":{"A":"A protein containing at least two peptide bonds.","B":"A reducing sugar, which has reduced copper(II) ions","C":"A lipid, which has formed a cloudy white emulsion.","D":"Starch, which has formed a blue-black complex with the reagent."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"Benedict's test: add reagent, heat in a water bath. Reducing sugars (all monosaccharides, plus maltose and lactose) reduce blue Cu²⁺ to brick-red Cu₂O. Colour sequence with increasing concentration: blue → green → yellow → orange → brick-red, so the test is semi-quantitative. Non-reducing sugars (sucrose) give no colour change until boiled with acid first, then neutralised, and re-tested. Starch uses iodine, not Benedict's.","reforge":{"stem":"A solution gives a negative Benedict's result, but after boiling with hydrochloric acid and neutralising it gives a brick-red precipitate. The solution contained:","options":{"A":"A lipid such as a triglyceride.","B":"A protein with several peptide bonds.","C":"Starch, which was broken down into maltose by the acid.","D":"A non-reducing sugar such as sucrose, now hydrolysed"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-18","stem":"The biuret test detects proteins by producing which colour change?","options":{"A":"Blue to blue-black on addition of the reagent.","B":"Colourless to a cloudy white emulsion after shaking.","C":"Blue to purple or lilac, with no heating required","D":"Blue to brick-red when heated in a water bath."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"Biuret: sodium hydroxide then dilute copper(II) sulfate (or ready-mixed reagent). Peptide bonds form a violet complex with Cu²⁺ — pale blue → purple/lilac. No heating needed, which distinguishes it from Benedict's. Colour intensity increases with protein concentration. Summary of the four standard tests: starch — iodine (blue-black); reducing sugar — Benedict's (brick-red); protein — biuret (purple); lipid — emulsion test (white emulsion).","reforge":{"stem":"Which observation would confirm the presence of a lipid in a food sample?","options":{"A":"A white emulsion forms when ethanol extract meets water","B":"A purple colour develops on adding biuret reagent.","C":"A blue-black colour develops on adding iodine solution.","D":"A brick-red precipitate forms on heating with Benedict's reagent."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.2"},{"id":"BIO-N1-19","stem":"An RNA nucleotide differs from a DNA nucleotide in that RNA contains:","options":{"A":"Deoxyribose sugar and the base thymine.","B":"Two complementary polynucleotide strands wound into a double helix.","C":"A phosphate group, which DNA nucleotides lack entirely.","D":"Ribose sugar and the base uracil in place of thymine"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"DNA: deoxyribose, bases A T C G, double-stranded antiparallel helix, stable long-term store of information. RNA: ribose (extra OH at carbon 2), bases A U C G, usually single-stranded, shorter-lived. Both are polymers of nucleotides (pentose + phosphate + base) joined by phosphodiester bonds. Option D describes DNA's structure rather than a nucleotide difference — read carefully whether a question asks about the nucleotide or the whole molecule.","reforge":{"stem":"In a DNA molecule, the two strands are held together by:","options":{"A":"Phosphodiester bonds between adjacent bases on opposite strands.","B":"Hydrogen bonds between base pairs, two A-T and three C-G","C":"Disulfide bridges between complementary purine bases.","D":"Ester bonds between deoxyribose and phosphate groups."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.3"},{"id":"BIO-N1-20","stem":"During transcription, the enzyme RNA polymerase:","options":{"A":"Builds mRNA complementary to the template DNA strand","B":"Removes RNA primers and replaces them with DNA nucleotides.","C":"Joins Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand of replicating DNA.","D":"Joins amino acids together to form a growing polypeptide chain."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"Transcription (nucleus): DNA helix unwinds, RNA polymerase reads the template (antisense) strand 3'→5' and builds mRNA 5'→3' using complementary base pairing, with uracil opposite adenine. In eukaryotes the pre-mRNA is spliced — introns removed, exons joined — before leaving through a nuclear pore. Translation (ribosome) then reads mRNA codons, with tRNA anticodons delivering amino acids. Keep the two processes and their enzymes distinct.","reforge":{"stem":"A DNA template strand reads TAC GGA. What is the corresponding mRNA sequence?","options":{"A":"TAC GGA, identical to the template strand.","B":"ATG CCT, matching the coding strand exactly.","C":"AUG CCU, with uracil substituted for thymine","D":"UAC GGA, with the sequence otherwise unchanged."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-21","stem":"A tRNA molecule's anticodon is complementary to:","options":{"A":"The entire template strand of the transcribed gene.","B":"A codon of three bases on the messenger RNA strand","C":"The amino acid it carries at its attachment site.","D":"The ribosomal RNA that forms the small ribosomal subunit."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"Translation: mRNA binds the small ribosomal subunit; tRNA with an anticodon complementary to the mRNA codon binds at the ribosome's binding sites, bringing a specific amino acid; a peptide bond forms; the ribosome moves one codon along; the tRNA is released and recycled. Each tRNA is specific to one amino acid. Start codon AUG (methionine); stop codons UAA, UAG, UGA carry no amino acid and end translation.","reforge":{"stem":"The genetic code is described as degenerate. This means that:","options":{"A":"Some codons in the mRNA are never translated by any organism.","B":"The code differs substantially between bacteria and mammals.","C":"Each codon can specify several different amino acids.","D":"Most amino acids are coded for by more than one codon"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-22","stem":"During which stage of mitosis do chromatids separate and move to opposite poles?","options":{"A":"Prophase, as the nuclear envelope breaks down.","B":"Metaphase, as chromosomes line up on the equator.","C":"Anaphase, as spindle fibres shorten and pull","D":"Telophase, as two new nuclear envelopes re-form."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-DIVISION","scaffold":"Mitosis (PMAT): Prophase — chromosomes condense and become visible as two sister chromatids, nuclear envelope breaks down, spindle forms. Metaphase — chromosomes align on the equator, attached by centromeres to spindle fibres. Anaphase — centromeres divide, spindle fibres shorten, chromatids pulled to opposite poles (requires ATP). Telophase — chromosomes decondense, nuclear envelopes re-form. Cytokinesis then divides the cytoplasm.","reforge":{"stem":"A drug prevents spindle fibres from shortening. At which stage would dividing cells become arrested?","options":{"A":"Anaphase, because chromatids could not be pulled apart","B":"Prophase, before the nuclear envelope breaks down.","C":"Interphase, before DNA replication begins.","D":"Telophase, because nuclear envelopes could not re-form."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.6"},{"id":"BIO-N1-23","stem":"Crossing over during meiosis increases genetic variation because it:","options":{"A":"Doubles the number of chromosomes present in each gamete.","B":"Introduces entirely new base sequences by copying errors in the DNA.","C":"Causes every gamete produced to be genetically identical.","D":"Exchanges alleles between non-sister chromatids of homologues"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-DIVISION","scaffold":"Meiosis generates variation in three ways: (1) crossing over in prophase I — homologous chromosomes pair as bivalents, chiasmata form, and non-sister chromatids exchange equivalent segments, producing new allele combinations on a chromosome; (2) independent assortment in metaphase I and II — the random orientation of each bivalent gives 2ⁿ combinations; (3) random fertilisation. Mutation is a separate source and is not part of meiosis itself.","reforge":{"stem":"A cell with 4 pairs of homologous chromosomes undergoes meiosis. How many different combinations arise from independent assortment alone?","options":{"A":"8, calculated as 4 × 2 for the two divisions.","B":"16, calculated as 2⁴ for four pairs","C":"4, one for each homologous pair present.","D":"256, calculated as 4⁴ for four pairs."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.2"},{"id":"BIO-N1-24","stem":"Checkpoints in the cell cycle are important because they:","options":{"A":"Verify DNA has replicated correctly before division","B":"Ensure that every daughter cell receives a haploid chromosome set.","C":"Provide the ATP required for spindle fibre movement in anaphase.","D":"Increase the rate of division in all healthy body tissues."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-DIVISION","scaffold":"The cell cycle is interphase (G1 — growth and protein synthesis; S — DNA replication; G2 — further growth and checking) then mitosis and cytokinesis. Checkpoints: G1/S checks cell size, nutrients and DNA damage; G2/M checks DNA is fully and correctly replicated; the spindle assembly checkpoint in metaphase checks all chromosomes are attached. Failure of checkpoint control allows damaged cells to divide, which underlies tumour formation.","reforge":{"stem":"Many cancer treatments target rapidly dividing cells. Why does this cause side effects such as hair loss?","options":{"A":"Hair follicle cells contain unusually high numbers of mitochondria.","B":"The drugs are transported preferentially to the scalp in the blood.","C":"Hair follicle cells also divide rapidly, so they are affected too","D":"Hair follicle cells lack cell cycle checkpoints altogether."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-25","stem":"A totipotent stem cell differs from a pluripotent stem cell because a totipotent cell can:","options":{"A":"Divide only a limited number of times before dying.","B":"Form any cell type, including extra-embryonic tissue","C":"Be obtained only from adult bone marrow samples.","D":"Differentiate into blood cells but no other tissue type."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-DIVISION","scaffold":"Potency scale: totipotent — can form any cell type plus placental/extra-embryonic tissue (only the zygote and first few divisions); pluripotent — any cell type of the body but not extra-embryonic (embryonic stem cells, iPS cells); multipotent — a limited range within a lineage (haematopoietic stem cells in bone marrow); unipotent — one type only (cardiomyocyte precursors). Differentiation happens because different genes are expressed, not because genes are lost.","reforge":{"stem":"All body cells contain the same genes, yet a neurone differs from a liver cell because:","options":{"A":"Liver cells have lost the genes needed to form neurones.","B":"Neurones contain extra chromosomes acquired during differentiation.","C":"The genetic code is read in a different direction in each tissue.","D":"Different genes are transcribed and translated in each cell type"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.4"},{"id":"BIO-N1-26","stem":"Osmosis is best defined as the movement of water molecules:","options":{"A":"Against a water potential gradient using energy from ATP.","B":"From a lower to a higher water potential through a membrane.","C":"From higher to lower water potential across a partially permeable membrane","D":"Through protein channels only, and never through the phospholipid bilayer."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Water potential (Ψ) is measured in kPa; pure water is 0 and all solutions are negative. Water moves down the gradient, from less negative (higher Ψ) to more negative (lower Ψ). Adding solute lowers Ψ. Osmosis is passive — no ATP. In plant cells, pressure potential from the wall opposes water entry; the cell becomes turgid. In animal cells there is no wall, so excess water entry causes lysis. Saying water moves 'to higher concentration' inverts the rule — think water potential, not solute concentration.","reforge":{"stem":"A plant cell with a water potential of −600 kPa is placed in a solution of −400 kPa. What happens?","options":{"A":"Water enters, because the solution has the higher water potential","B":"Water leaves the cell, because the solution is more concentrated.","C":"No net movement occurs, because both values are negative.","D":"Water leaves the cell and the cell membrane pulls away from the wall."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.5"},{"id":"BIO-N1-27","stem":"Active transport differs from facilitated diffusion because active transport:","options":{"A":"Uses channel proteins rather than any carrier proteins.","B":"Moves substances down their concentration gradient more quickly.","C":"Occurs only across the membranes of plant cells.","D":"Requires ATP and moves substances against the gradient"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Passive processes (no ATP, down the gradient): simple diffusion (small non-polar — O₂, CO₂), facilitated diffusion (channel or carrier proteins for ions, glucose, amino acids), osmosis (water, partly via aquaporins). Active transport: carrier protein changes shape using ATP hydrolysis, moves substances against the gradient — e.g. the sodium-potassium pump, mineral uptake by root hairs. Both facilitated diffusion and active transport use proteins; the ATP and the gradient direction are what separate them.","reforge":{"stem":"Cells lining the small intestine are treated with a respiratory inhibitor. Glucose uptake by active transport stops because:","options":{"A":"Carrier proteins are denatured by the inhibitor directly.","B":"No ATP is produced, so carrier proteins cannot change shape","C":"The concentration gradient of glucose is immediately reversed.","D":"The phospholipid bilayer becomes impermeable to all solutes."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.5"},{"id":"BIO-N1-28","stem":"Cholesterol in an animal cell surface membrane functions to:","options":{"A":"Regulate fluidity by packing between phospholipid tails","B":"Transport ions across the membrane down their electrochemical gradient.","C":"Provide the cell with a store of energy for aerobic respiration.","D":"Act as a receptor site for peptide hormones such as insulin."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Membrane components and roles: phospholipids — the bilayer itself; cholesterol — sits between the fatty acid tails, restricting movement at higher temperatures and preventing tight packing at low temperatures, so it buffers fluidity and reduces permeability to ions; glycoproteins and glycolipids — cell recognition, receptors and adhesion; channel and carrier proteins — transport; enzymes. Cholesterol is a steroid lipid, not a protein, so it cannot act as a hormone receptor.","reforge":{"stem":"Beetroot cells release pigment when placed in solvent or at high temperature. This is because both treatments:","options":{"A":"Increase the rate of active transport out of the vacuole.","B":"Cause the cells to become turgid and burst open.","C":"Disrupt membrane structure, increasing permeability","D":"Convert cholesterol in the membrane into phospholipid."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.5"},{"id":"BIO-N1-29","stem":"Phagocytosis of a bacterium by a neutrophil involves:","options":{"A":"Diffusion of the bacterium through channel proteins in the membrane.","B":"Engulfing the bacterium into a vesicle that fuses with a lysosome","C":"Active transport of bacterial proteins across the cell surface membrane.","D":"Osmotic entry of the bacterium down a water potential gradient."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Phagocytosis is bulk transport, not diffusion — pathogens are far too large for membrane proteins. Sequence: chemicals released by the pathogen attract the phagocyte (chemotaxis); receptors bind to antigens or attached antibodies (opsonins); the membrane engulfs the pathogen forming a phagosome; lysosomes fuse to form a phagolysosome; hydrolytic enzymes digest it; harmless products are absorbed and antigens may be displayed on the surface. Endocytosis and exocytosis both require ATP.","reforge":{"stem":"After a phagocyte digests a pathogen, it may display the pathogen's antigens on its own surface. This cell is then acting as:","options":{"A":"A memory cell, providing long-term immunity to reinfection.","B":"A plasma cell, secreting large quantities of antibody.","C":"A red blood cell, transporting antigens around the body.","D":"An antigen-presenting cell, activating T lymphocytes"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-30","stem":"A student uses an eyepiece graticule to measure cells. The graticule must first be calibrated with a stage micrometer because:","options":{"A":"The stage micrometer magnifies the specimen further.","B":"The graticule alters the resolution of the objective lens.","C":"The graticule scale has no fixed units of its own","D":"Cells change size when viewed under different objective lenses."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"An eyepiece graticule is an arbitrary scale in the eyepiece; its divisions represent a different real distance at each objective magnification. A stage micrometer is a slide with a known scale (usually 1mm divided into 100 divisions of 10μm). Calibration: line the two scales up, count how many graticule divisions match a known micrometer length, and calculate μm per division. Recalibrate whenever the objective is changed.","reforge":{"stem":"At ×400, 25 eyepiece graticule divisions align with 0.25mm on the stage micrometer. One graticule division equals:","options":{"A":"10 μm, because 0.25mm is 250 μm and 250 ÷ 25 = 10","B":"0.01 μm, because 0.25 divided by 25 equals 0.01.","C":"100 μm, because the magnification is ×400 at this setting.","D":"25 μm, because 0.25mm divided by 25 gives 25 μm."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-31","stem":"A student views a slide of onion epidermis and sees cells that have shrunk away from the cell wall. The cells have been placed in a solution that is:","options":{"A":"Facilitated diffusion is too slow for the rate of glucose absorption needed after a meal.","B":"Hypotonic, causing the cells to take up water and swell.","C":"Pure water, giving the highest possible water potential.","D":"Hypertonic, so water has left the cells and they are plasmolysed"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Plasmolysis: in a solution of lower water potential than the cell, water leaves by osmosis, the protoplast shrinks and pulls away from the cell wall. Incipient plasmolysis is the point at which the membrane just begins to detach, and is used to estimate the cell's own water potential. In a hypotonic solution the reverse happens: water enters, the cell becomes turgid, and the wall prevents bursting — unlike an animal cell, which lyses.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does an animal cell burst in pure water while a plant cell does not?","options":{"A":"The animal cell has a higher water potential than the plant cell.","B":"The plant cell wall resists expansion and prevents bursting","C":"The animal cell membrane is impermeable to water molecules.","D":"The plant cell actively pumps the excess water back out."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-N1-32","stem":"A gene mutation substitutes one base for another, but the resulting protein is unchanged. This is because the genetic code is:","options":{"A":"Degenerate, so more than one codon can specify the same amino acid","B":"Replication only occurs during the S phase for some chromosomes, not all.","C":"Non-overlapping, so each base is read only once.","D":"Read in triplets from a fixed starting point on the strand."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"Substitution mutations can be silent (a degenerate code means the new codon still specifies the same amino acid), missense (a different amino acid, which may alter the tertiary structure and function), or nonsense (a premature stop codon, truncating the protein). Insertions and deletions are usually more damaging because they cause a frameshift, changing every codon downstream. Which effect occurs depends on the position and the type of change.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a single base deletion usually more damaging than a single base substitution?","options":{"A":"Deletions always remove an entire gene from the chromosome.","B":"Substitutions cannot change the amino acid that is coded for.","C":"It causes a frameshift, altering every codon that follows","D":"Deletions prevent the DNA from being replicated at all."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-1","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.2"}]},"BIO-2":{"label":"Cells, Transport & Enzymes","color":"#16a34a","questions":[{"id":"BIO-09","stem":"A student states that osmosis is 'the movement of water from low to high concentration'. Why is this imprecise?","options":{"A":"Osmosis is the net movement of water from higher to lower water potential across a partially permeable membrane","B":"Osmosis moves solutes from low to high concentration across a membrane.","C":"Osmosis is active transport of water, requiring ATP to move against the gradient.","D":"It removes the need for identification in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Precise definition: net movement of water molecules from higher to lower WATER POTENTIAL across a partially permeable membrane. Water potential (ψ) is measured in kPa; pure water = 0, solutions are negative. 'Concentration' is ambiguous — water moves from high water concentration (dilute) to low water concentration (concentrated solute), i.e. down its water potential gradient. Osmosis is passive (no ATP). Marks are lost for omitting 'partially permeable membrane' or 'water potential'.","reforge":{"stem":"A plant cell is placed in pure water. What happens and why?","options":{"A":"Water leaves the cell because the cell has lower water potential; it becomes flaccid.","B":"Nothing happens — pure water has no effect on cells.","C":"Water enters by osmosis and the cell becomes turgid as the wall resists","D":"The cell bursts because plant cells have no wall."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.5"},{"id":"BIO-10","stem":"What distinguishes active transport from facilitated diffusion?","options":{"A":"Active transport moves only water molecules; facilitated diffusion handles all other ions.","B":"Active transport uses ATP and moves substances against the concentration gradient","C":"Facilitated diffusion requires ATP to open channel proteins; active transport is entirely passive.","D":"Active transport and facilitated diffusion are two names for the same membrane process."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Facilitated diffusion: passive, down the gradient, via channel or carrier proteins, no ATP. Active transport: against the gradient, requires ATP and carrier proteins that change shape. Both use membrane proteins — the difference is energy and direction. Co-transport (e.g. glucose-sodium in the ileum) links the two. Common error: thinking any protein-mediated transport is 'active'. Simple diffusion needs no protein at all (small non-polar molecules like O2, CO2).","reforge":{"stem":"Glucose is absorbed from the gut lumen into epithelial cells even when its concentration is already higher inside the cell. This is possible because of:","options":{"A":"Simple diffusion through the phospholipid bilayer.","B":"Facilitated diffusion down the glucose gradient.","C":"Osmosis pulling glucose along with water.","D":"Sodium moving down its gradient drags glucose"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.5"},{"id":"BIO-11","stem":"A student explains enzyme action using the 'lock and key' model exclusively. What does the induced fit model add?","options":{"A":"Induced fit means enzymes are used up and must be replaced during each reaction.","B":"Muscle temperature always falls during exercise in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology","C":"Induced fit: the active site flexes around the substrate on binding, straining bonds and lowering activation energy.","D":"Induced fit means one enzyme can catalyse any substrate because the site is adaptable."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Lock and key: rigid complementary active site. Induced fit (the accepted model): the active site is flexible and moulds around the substrate on binding, straining substrate bonds and stabilising the transition state → lowers activation energy. Enzymes are catalysts (not used up), highly specific (complementary tertiary structure), and lower Ea without changing ΔG. Common error: describing the active site as 'exactly the same shape as the substrate' — it is complementary and dynamic, not identical.","reforge":{"stem":"How does an enzyme increase the rate of a reaction?","options":{"A":"By lowering the activation energy required","B":"By raising the temperature of the surroundings.","C":"By being consumed to provide energy for the reaction.","D":"By increasing the activation energy required."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-12","stem":"Above its optimum temperature, an enzyme's rate falls sharply. What is the correct explanation?","options":{"A":"The substrate molecules evaporate at high temperature, leaving nothing to react.","B":"The enzyme is consumed faster at high temperature, depleting the catalyst.","C":"The enzyme moves too fast to form stable collisions with the substrate.","D":"Vibration above the optimum breaks bonds in the tertiary structure"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Below optimum: rate rises with temperature (more kinetic energy, more frequent successful collisions). At optimum: maximum rate. Above optimum: thermal vibration breaks the hydrogen/ionic bonds holding the tertiary structure → active site distorts → substrate no longer fits → denaturation (usually irreversible). Key phrasing: 'active site changes shape and is no longer complementary'. Common error: saying the enzyme 'dies' or 'melts' — enzymes are not alive; use 'denatures'. Extreme pH denatures similarly.","reforge":{"stem":"A denatured enzyme cannot catalyse its reaction because:","options":{"A":"It has been chemically converted into its substrate.","B":"Its active site shape has changed, so substrate no longer fits","C":"It has run out of energy.","D":"Its primary structure (amino acid sequence) has been permanently rearranged."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-13","stem":"Which feature is found in a prokaryotic cell but NOT in a eukaryotic cell?","options":{"A":"A plasmid — a small circular DNA loop separate from the main chromosome.","B":"Ribosomes, which are present in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.","C":"A cell surface membrane, present in all living cells including prokaryotes.","D":"A cell wall, found in bacteria, plants and fungi but not animal cells."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-STRUCTURE","scaffold":"Prokaryotic features: no membrane-bound organelles, no true nucleus (circular DNA free in cytoplasm), plasmids, smaller (70S) ribosomes, murein/peptidoglycan cell wall, sometimes capsule/flagella. Eukaryotic: nucleus, membrane-bound organelles (mitochondria, ER, Golgi), 80S ribosomes. Shared: cell membrane, cytoplasm, ribosomes, DNA. Traps: cell walls exist in BOTH (different materials); ribosomes exist in both (different sizes). Only plasmids, circular free DNA, and 70S ribosomes are prokaryote-distinctive here.","reforge":{"stem":"Both prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells contain ribosomes. What is the difference?","options":{"A":"A fixed arrangement of cholesterol molecules with protein channels at regular intervals.","B":"Only prokaryotes have ribosomes.","C":"Prokaryotic ribosomes are 70S (smaller); eukaryotic ribosomes are 80S (larger).","D":"Eukaryotic ribosomes are made of DNA; prokaryotic of RNA."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.1"},{"id":"BIO-14","stem":"A student claims a large organism 'has a large surface area to volume ratio, so it needs a transport system'. Correct the reasoning.","options":{"A":"Large organisms have a large ratio and can diffuse everything directly through the body surface.","B":"Large organisms have a SMALL SA:V ratio — diffusion alone cannot meet their needs","C":"Surface area to volume ratio stays constant regardless of organism size.","D":"Transport systems actively reduce the surface area to volume ratio in large organisms."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"As size increases, VOLUME grows faster than SURFACE AREA, so the SA:V ratio DECREASES. Small organisms (large SA:V) exchange by diffusion across the body surface. Large organisms (small SA:V) have longer diffusion distances and higher demands → need specialised exchange surfaces (alveoli, gills, villi — all increasing SA) and mass transport (circulatory) systems. Common error: reversing the direction of the ratio. Exchange surface adaptations: large SA, thin, moist, good blood supply, ventilation.","reforge":{"stem":"Alveoli, gills and root hair cells share which key adaptation for efficient exchange?","options":{"A":"They actively transport all substances using ATP.","B":"They are impermeable to prevent water loss.","C":"They are located deep inside the organism away from the environment.","D":"A large surface area with a thin, short diffusion pathway"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-15","stem":"During the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle), what is the role of RuBisCO?","options":{"A":"Muscle temperature always falls during exercise in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology","B":"RuBisCO absorbs light energy to excite electrons in the chlorophyll molecule.","C":"RuBisCO fixes CO2 onto RuBP to form glycerate-3-phosphate — the entry point of the Calvin cycle.","D":"RuBisCO produces ATP directly by phosphorylating ADP during the dark reaction."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-RESPIRATION-PHOTOSYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Calvin cycle (stroma, light-independent): CO2 + RuBP (5C) → 2× GP (3C), catalysed by RuBisCO. GP is reduced to triose phosphate (TP) using reduced NADP and ATP from the light-dependent reactions. TP forms glucose and regenerates RuBP. Water splitting (photolysis) and ATP/NADPH production happen in the LIGHT-DEPENDENT stage (thylakoids). Common error: placing photolysis or light absorption in the Calvin cycle, or forgetting the cycle depends on products of the light stage.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the Calvin cycle stop shortly after light is removed, even though it is called 'light-independent'?","options":{"A":"It needs ATP and reduced NADP","B":"CO2 is only available in the light.","C":"RuBisCO requires light to function.","D":"The stroma freezes in the dark."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.6"},{"id":"BIO-16","stem":"Two alleles, one for brown eyes (B) and one for blue (b), where B is dominant. A student says a brown-eyed parent 'must be BB'. Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"Brown eyes are actually recessive — only one allele is needed to suppress blue.","B":"Discontinuous variation is always caused by genes; continuous variation is always caused by the environment.","C":"Eye colour is determined by environment, not genetics.","D":"A brown-eyed person could be BB or Bb — dominant phenotype cannot confirm genotype without a test cross."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"Dominant phenotype = at least one dominant allele → genotype is BB or Bb (unknown without further info). Recessive phenotype = must be homozygous recessive (bb). This is why two brown-eyed (Bb × Bb) parents can have a blue-eyed child (1/4 bb). Terms: allele (version of a gene), homozygous (same alleles), heterozygous (different), genotype (alleles present), phenotype (observable trait). A test cross with a homozygous recessive reveals an unknown dominant genotype.","reforge":{"stem":"Two brown-eyed parents have a blue-eyed child. What must the parents' genotypes be?","options":{"A":"Both BB — brown-eyed parents are always homozygous.","B":"Both Bb (heterozygous) — each carries a recessive b allele, so a bb child is possible with probability 1/4.","C":"It removes the need for identification in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology","D":"One BB and one bb."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.2"},{"id":"BIO-E2-01","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"A steep water potential gradient across a root hair membrane tends to:","options":{"A":"increase net water entry by osmosis","B":"stop all water movement","C":"Only moves water molecules across the phospholipid bilayer.","D":"destroy the cell wall"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Water moves from higher to lower water potential across a partially permeable membrane.","reforge":{"stem":"Root hair cells sit in soil water with a higher water potential than the cell's cytoplasm. What does this water potential gradient cause?","options":{"A":"Net water entry into the root hair cell by osmosis","B":"A complete stop to all water movement","C":"Sucrose moving out of the cell into the soil in a typical cell","D":"Destruction of the cell wall"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.3"},{"id":"BIO-E2-02","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"Transpiration is the loss of:","options":{"A":"Arteries actively pumping the blood themselves in a typical cell","B":"water vapour from a plant's aerial parts","C":"sucrose from phloem","D":"oxygen from mitochondria"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Most transpiration occurs through stomata in leaves.","reforge":{"stem":"On a hot day, a plant's leaves lose a measurable amount of moisture through open stomata into the air. What is this loss of water vapour from a plant's aerial parts called?","options":{"A":"The loss of carbon dioxide from roots only","B":"Transpiration","C":"The loss of sucrose from phloem","D":"The loss of oxygen from mitochondria"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.3"},{"id":"BIO-E2-03","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"The mass flow hypothesis explains transport in:","options":{"A":"the nucleus","B":"the alveoli","C":"the phloem","D":"the xylem only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Pressure differences drive movement of assimilates through sieve tubes.","reforge":{"stem":"Sugars produced in a leaf are transported down the stem to the roots along a pressure gradient created by loading and unloading sucrose. Which tissue, and which hypothesis, explains this transport?","options":{"A":"The nucleus, explained by osmotic diffusion","B":"The alveoli, explained by gas exchange","C":"The phloem, explained by the mass flow hypothesis","D":"The xylem only, explained by transpiration pull in a typical cell"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.3"},{"id":"BIO-E2-04","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"An enzyme's active site is important because it:","options":{"A":"Oxidised — the enzyme loses electrons at high temperature, altering its charge.","B":"always works at any pH","C":"is made from cellulose","D":"has a complementary shape to its substrate"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Specific interactions between active site and substrate determine enzyme action.","reforge":{"stem":"A particular enzyme will only catalyse a reaction involving one specific substrate molecule, and no other. What property of the enzyme's active site explains this specificity?","options":{"A":"It contains the cell's DNA in a typical cell in this biological system","B":"It always works at any pH","C":"It is made from cellulose","D":"It has a shape complementary to that particular substrate"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-GAP2-01","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"The main function of haemoglobin in red blood cells is to:","options":{"A":"carry oxygen reversibly using haem groups","B":"digest glucose in plasma","C":"Valves creating oxygen as blood passes through","D":"form cellulose fibres"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Oxygen binds reversibly to haem groups, allowing loading and unloading.","reforge":{"stem":"Red blood cells transport oxygen from the lungs to respiring tissues, releasing it again where it is needed. Which molecule carries out this role, and how?","options":{"A":"Haemoglobin, carrying oxygen reversibly using haem groups","B":"An enzyme that digests glucose in the plasma in a typical cell","C":"An antibody produced in the alveoli","D":"A protein that forms cellulose fibres"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-GAP2-02","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"A capillary is well suited to exchange because it has:","options":{"A":"The xylem only, explained by transpiration pull in a typical cell","B":"a thin wall and a large network close to cells","C":"valves along its entire length","D":"no concentration gradients"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Short diffusion distances and extensive branching support exchange.","reforge":{"stem":"Substances such as oxygen and glucose diffuse quickly between blood and body tissues at capillaries but not at arteries. Which structural features of capillaries explain this?","options":{"A":"Thick muscle and a single opening","B":"A thin wall and a large network of vessels close to cells","C":"Valves running along the entire vessel","D":"The complete absence of any concentration gradients in a typical cell"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-GAP2-03","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"The Bohr effect helps oxygen delivery when respiring tissues have:","options":{"A":"no carbon dioxide and higher pH","B":"An enzyme that digests glucose in the plasma in a typical cell","C":"higher carbon dioxide concentration and lower pH","D":"fewer haemoglobin molecules only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Carbon dioxide lowers pH, reducing haemoglobin affinity and promoting oxygen unloading.","reforge":{"stem":"Haemoglobin releases oxygen more readily in actively respiring muscle tissue than in the lungs. Which local conditions in that tissue cause this increased release?","options":{"A":"No carbon dioxide and a higher pH","B":"Lower temperature and no blood flow","C":"Higher carbon dioxide concentration and lower pH","D":"Simply fewer haemoglobin molecules present in a typical cell"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-GAP2-04","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"In a mammal, the pulmonary artery carries:","options":{"A":"The complete absence of any concentration gradients in a typical cell","B":"blood from the lungs to the aorta","C":"lymph to the right atrium","D":"deoxygenated blood from the heart to the lungs"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"It carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs.","reforge":{"stem":"Blood leaving the right ventricle of the heart travels to the lungs to be oxygenated. Which vessel carries this blood, and in what state?","options":{"A":"The pulmonary artery, carrying oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart in a typical cell","B":"A lymph vessel, carrying fluid to the right atrium","C":"The pulmonary artery, carrying blood from the lungs to the aorta","D":"The pulmonary artery, carrying deoxygenated blood from the heart to the lungs"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-GAP2-05","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"A tissue fluid forms when:","options":{"A":"hydrostatic pressure forces some plasma out of capillaries","B":"red blood cells leave every artery","C":"lymph enters the alveoli","D":"Simply fewer haemoglobin molecules present in a typical cell"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Pressure at the arteriole end drives fluid out through capillary walls.","reforge":{"stem":"Fluid is pushed out of capillaries into the spaces between cells, bathing the surrounding tissue. What causes this fluid to leave the capillaries in the first place?","options":{"A":"Hydrostatic pressure forcing some plasma out of the capillary wall","B":"Red blood cells leaving every artery","C":"Lymph entering the alveoli","D":"Haemoglobin being filtered by the liver in a typical cell in this biological system"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-GAP2-06","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"Double circulation is advantageous because it:","options":{"A":"Haemoglobin being filtered by the liver in a typical cell in this biological system","B":"allows high pressure to reach the body after blood is re-pressurised","C":"removes the need for capillaries","D":"stops gas exchange in the lungs"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Separate pulmonary and systemic circuits support efficient delivery to tissues.","reforge":{"stem":"In a mammal, blood passes through the heart twice on each full circuit of the body, once for the lungs and once for the rest of the body. What is the main advantage of this arrangement?","options":{"A":"It mixes oxygenated and deoxygenated blood completely in a typical cell","B":"Blood can be re-pressurised so it reaches the body at high pressure","C":"It removes the need for capillaries entirely","D":"It stops gas exchange from happening in the lungs"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-01","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"A homeostatic control system usually includes:","options":{"A":"a pathogen and an antibody","B":"A mechanism that amplifies every deviation indefinitely in a typical cell","C":"a receptor, a coordinator and an effector","D":"two identical enzymes"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Receptors detect change, coordinators process information and effectors act.","reforge":{"stem":"A thermoregulation system in the body detects a change in temperature, processes the information, and triggers sweating or shivering in response. What three components does a homeostatic control system like this typically include?","options":{"A":"A pathogen and an antibody only in a typical cell","B":"Only a stimulus and a response","C":"A receptor, a coordinator and an effector","D":"Two identical enzymes"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-02","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"Negative feedback maintains a variable by:","options":{"A":"The number of red blood cells made in bone marrow in a typical cell","B":"preventing any response to change","C":"removing all receptors","D":"reversing a change and returning the system towards its set point"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"The response reduces the original deviation from the normal range.","reforge":{"stem":"Blood glucose rises after a meal, triggering insulin release, which then brings glucose back down towards its normal level. What mechanism does this describe?","options":{"A":"A mechanism that amplifies every deviation indefinitely in a typical cell","B":"A mechanism that prevents any response to change","C":"A mechanism that removes all receptors involved","D":"Negative feedback, reversing a change and returning towards the set point"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.1"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-03","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"The kidney contributes to homeostasis by regulating:","options":{"A":"water balance and the excretion of nitrogenous waste","B":"Active transport removing all proteins from the blood in a typical cell","C":"only oxygen concentration in alveoli","D":"light absorption in chloroplasts"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Ultrafiltration and selective reabsorption help control blood composition.","reforge":{"stem":"The kidneys filter blood and adjust how much water and urea leave the body in urine. Which two aspects of homeostasis does this most directly regulate?","options":{"A":"Water balance and the excretion of nitrogenous waste","B":"The number of red blood cells made in bone marrow in a typical cell","C":"Only the oxygen concentration in the alveoli","D":"Light absorption in chloroplasts"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.1"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-04","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"Ultrafiltration occurs in the renal corpuscle because:","options":{"A":"All sodium permanently leaving the axon in a typical cell in this biological system","B":"high pressure forces small solutes through a filtration barrier","C":"collecting ducts secrete glucose","D":"urea is produced by the nephron"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Small molecules pass through while cells and most plasma proteins remain in the blood.","reforge":{"stem":"In the renal corpuscle, water, glucose, salts and urea pass out of the blood into the nephron, while proteins and blood cells stay behind. What forces this filtration to happen?","options":{"A":"Active transport removing all proteins from the blood in a typical cell","B":"High pressure forcing small solutes through a filtration barrier","C":"Collecting ducts secreting glucose into the blood","D":"Urea being produced directly by the nephron"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.2"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-05","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"An action potential is generated when:","options":{"A":"the synapse releases antibodies","B":"A hormone that causes red blood cells to leave the nephron in a typical cell","C":"voltage-gated sodium channels open after threshold is reached","D":"myelin becomes a neurotransmitter"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Sodium ion influx depolarises the membrane during the rising phase.","reforge":{"stem":"A neurone's membrane potential suddenly reverses and spikes when a stimulus is strong enough to reach a critical level. What triggers this electrical event?","options":{"A":"The synapse releasing antibodies","B":"All sodium permanently leaving the axon in a typical cell in this biological system","C":"Voltage-gated sodium channels opening once the threshold is reached","D":"Myelin acting as a neurotransmitter"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.3"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-06","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"Saltatory conduction is rapid because an action potential:","options":{"A":"By promoting glycogen breakdown in the liver only in a typical cell","B":"moves only across the cell body","C":"uses no ion channels","D":"jumps between nodes of Ranvier on a myelinated axon"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Myelin insulates internodes so depolarisation is regenerated at nodes.","reforge":{"stem":"A nerve impulse travels far faster along a myelinated axon than along an unmyelinated one of the same diameter. What allows this faster transmission?","options":{"A":"The impulse travelling through the blood plasma instead","B":"The impulse moving only across the cell body","C":"The impulse using no ion channels at all","D":"The impulse jumping between nodes of Ranvier"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.3"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-07","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"ADH increases water reabsorption by causing:","options":{"A":"aquaporins to be inserted into collecting-duct membranes","B":"red blood cells to leave the nephron","C":"the glomerulus to stop filtering","D":"Electrical impulses crossing without any chemicals involved in a typical cell"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"ADH increases collecting-duct permeability so more water enters the blood.","reforge":{"stem":"When the body is dehydrated, more water is reabsorbed from the collecting ducts back into the blood. Which hormone causes this, and how?","options":{"A":"ADH, causing aquaporins to be inserted into collecting-duct membranes","B":"A hormone that causes red blood cells to leave the nephron in a typical cell","C":"A hormone that stops the glomerulus from filtering","D":"A hormone that breaks down glucose in the ureter"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.4"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-08","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"Insulin lowers blood glucose concentration by promoting:","options":{"A":"The impulse travelling through the blood plasma instead","B":"glucose uptake and glycogen formation in target cells","C":"water loss from collecting ducts","D":"glucagon release from beta cells"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Insulin encourages cells to take up glucose and store it as glycogen.","reforge":{"stem":"After a carbohydrate-rich meal, blood glucose concentration rises and a hormone is released to bring it back down. How does this hormone lower blood glucose?","options":{"A":"By promoting glycogen breakdown in the liver only in a typical cell","B":"By promoting glucose uptake and glycogen formation in target cells","C":"By promoting water loss from the collecting ducts","D":"By promoting glucagon release from beta cells"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.4"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-09","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"A synapse ensures one-way transmission because:","options":{"A":"A mechanism that amplifies every deviation indefinitely in a typical cell","B":"both membranes release identical hormones","C":"neurotransmitter vesicles are presynaptic and receptors are postsynaptic","D":"the postsynaptic membrane has no proteins"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Vesicle release and receptor location establish direction at a chemical synapse.","reforge":{"stem":"An impulse can only cross a synapse in one direction, from the presynaptic neurone to the postsynaptic neurone. What structural feature ensures this one-way transmission?","options":{"A":"Electrical impulses crossing without any chemicals involved in a typical cell","B":"Both membranes releasing identical hormones","C":"Neurotransmitter vesicles being presynaptic while receptors are postsynaptic","D":"The postsynaptic membrane having no proteins at all"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.3"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-10","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"A cloning technique can be used to produce:","options":{"A":"An enzyme that destroys every plasmid in a cell in a typical cell","B":"proteins without transcription","C":"a population without any DNA","D":"genetically identical cells or organisms from one source"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETIC-ENGINEERING","scaffold":"Cloning copies genetic material, although environment can affect phenotype.","reforge":{"stem":"A scientist takes cells from a single adult sheep and uses them to produce several genetically identical lambs. What technique is being used?","options":{"A":"A technique producing only organisms with entirely new alleles in a typical cell","B":"A technique producing proteins without transcription","C":"A technique producing a population without any DNA","D":"Cloning, producing genetically identical cells or organisms from one source"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.4"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-11","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"A restriction enzyme is used in genetic engineering to:","options":{"A":"cut DNA at a specific base sequence","B":"only organisms with entirely new alleles","C":"join amino acids into proteins","D":"measure the rate of respiration"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETIC-ENGINEERING","scaffold":"Restriction enzymes create fragments with specific ends for DNA manipulation.","reforge":{"stem":"A biotechnologist needs to cut a gene out of a DNA molecule at an exact, specific point. Which type of enzyme is used for this, and how does it work?","options":{"A":"A restriction enzyme, cutting DNA at a specific base sequence","B":"An enzyme that destroys every plasmid in a cell in a typical cell","C":"An enzyme that joins amino acids into proteins","D":"An enzyme that measures the rate of respiration"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.3"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-12","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"A transgenic organism contains:","options":{"A":"A technique producing only organisms with entirely new alleles in a typical cell","B":"a gene introduced from another species or source","C":"an antibody instead of DNA","D":"only mitochondrial DNA"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETIC-ENGINEERING","scaffold":"The introduced gene can be expressed if suitable regulatory sequences are present.","reforge":{"stem":"A crop plant is genetically modified so that it now carries and expresses a gene originally taken from a bacterium. What term describes this modified plant?","options":{"A":"An organism with no genes in its chromosomes","B":"A transgenic organism, carrying a gene introduced from another species","C":"An organism with an antibody instead of DNA","D":"An organism containing only mitochondrial DNA in a typical cell in this biological system"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.3"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-13","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"In an ecosystem, decomposers are important because they:","options":{"A":"prevent nutrient cycling","B":"remove all energy from sunlight","C":"return mineral ions to the environment by breaking down organic matter","D":"Active transport of bacterial proteins across the cell surface membrane."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Decomposition releases inorganic nutrients that producers can reuse.","reforge":{"stem":"Fungi and bacteria break down dead organic matter in a woodland, releasing nutrients back into the soil. Why are these decomposers important to the ecosystem?","options":{"A":"They prevent nutrient cycling from occurring","B":"They remove all energy that arrives from sunlight in a typical cell in this biological system","C":"They return mineral ions to the environment by breaking down organic matter","D":"They convert nitrogen directly into glucose"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.5"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-14","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"Energy transfer between trophic levels is inefficient mainly because:","options":{"A":"energy is created by consumers","B":"plants cannot absorb light","C":"Diffusion of the bacterium through channel proteins in the membrane.","D":"energy is lost in respiration, movement, heat and uneaten material"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Only a fraction of consumed energy becomes biomass available to the next level.","reforge":{"stem":"Only around 10% of the energy available at one trophic level is typically passed on to the next level in a food chain. Why is this transfer so inefficient?","options":{"A":"Energy is created anew by consumers at each level in a typical cell in this biological system","B":"Plants are unable to absorb any light energy","C":"All biomass is transferred intact between levels","D":"Energy is lost through respiration, movement, heat and uneaten material"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.5"},{"id":"BIO-GAP3-15","spec":"BIO-2","stem":"A population reaches carrying capacity when:","options":{"A":"available resources limit its size and birth rate balances death rate","B":"resources are unlimited","C":"They remove all energy that arrives from sunlight in a typical cell in this biological system","D":"all organisms become genetically identical"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Competition and limiting factors prevent indefinite population growth.","reforge":{"stem":"A population of rabbits in a field grows quickly at first, then levels off once food and space become limited. What has this population reached?","options":{"A":"Its carrying capacity, where resources limit size and birth balances death","B":"A state where resources remain unlimited","C":"A state where every individual reproduces without any competition in a typical cell","D":"A state where all organisms become genetically identical"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.6"},{"id":"BIO-N2-01","stem":"Alveoli are adapted for efficient gas exchange mainly because they provide:","options":{"A":"A thick, muscular wall that actively pumps oxygen into the blood.","B":"A large surface area with a very short diffusion pathway","C":"A dry surface that prevents gases from dissolving before diffusion.","D":"A store of haemoglobin held within the alveolar epithelial cells."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Fick's law: rate of diffusion ∝ (surface area × concentration difference) ÷ thickness. Alveolar adaptations map onto each term — roughly 300 million alveoli give a huge surface area; the alveolar epithelium and capillary endothelium are each one flattened cell thick, so the pathway is ~0.3μm; continuous blood flow and ventilation maintain the concentration gradient. The moist lining is needed because gases must dissolve before diffusing.","reforge":{"stem":"Emphysema destroys the walls between alveoli, merging them into larger air spaces. Gas exchange becomes less efficient because:","options":{"A":"The diffusion pathway becomes shorter than normal.","B":"Haemoglobin can no longer bind oxygen molecules.","C":"The concentration gradient for oxygen is reversed.","D":"The surface area for diffusion is reduced"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-02","stem":"During inspiration in a mammal, the diaphragm and external intercostal muscles:","options":{"A":"Contract, decreasing thoracic volume and raising internal pressure.","B":"Relax, allowing elastic recoil to force air out of the alveoli.","C":"Contract, increasing thoracic volume and lowering lung pressure","D":"Remain unchanged, since air movement depends only on diffusion gradients."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Inspiration is active: diaphragm contracts and flattens, external intercostals contract pulling the ribcage up and out, thoracic volume increases, pressure falls below atmospheric, and air flows in down the pressure gradient. Normal expiration is largely passive: muscles relax, elastic recoil reduces volume, pressure rises, air flows out. Forced expiration adds contraction of the internal intercostals and abdominal muscles. Air always moves down a pressure gradient, not by diffusion.","reforge":{"stem":"Which measurement would be obtained using a spirometer during quiet breathing?","options":{"A":"Tidal volume, the volume of air moved in one normal breath","B":"The percentage saturation of haemoglobin in the alveolar capillaries.","C":"The number of alveoli recruited during each breath.","D":"Partial pressure of oxygen in the pulmonary artery."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-03","stem":"Fish gills use a countercurrent flow system, in which water and blood flow:","options":{"A":"In the same direction, so equilibrium is reached rapidly.","B":"In pulses that alternate between the two directions.","C":"At right angles, so oxygen diffuses across the shortest distance.","D":"In opposite directions, maintaining a gradient throughout"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Countercurrent: water flows across the gill lamellae in the opposite direction to blood flow in the capillaries. At every point along the lamella the water has a higher oxygen concentration than the adjacent blood, so a diffusion gradient is maintained across the entire length and roughly 80% of the oxygen is extracted. With parallel (concurrent) flow, the two would reach equilibrium halfway along and only ~50% could be absorbed.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does a parallel flow system extract less oxygen than a countercurrent system?","options":{"A":"Parallel flow requires more ATP to maintain the water current.","B":"Blood and water reach equilibrium partway along the lamella","C":"Parallel flow reduces the total surface area of the gill lamellae.","D":"Oxygen diffuses backwards into the water along the whole lamella."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-N2-04","stem":"Insects transport respiratory gases directly to their tissues using:","options":{"A":"A tracheal system of tubes opening at spiracles","B":"Haemoglobin dissolved in the haemolymph of the open circulation.","C":"Gill lamellae supported by a bony operculum.","D":"Alveoli ventilated by a muscular diaphragm."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Insects have no respiratory pigment for gas transport. Air enters through spiracles (which can close to limit water loss), passes along tracheae, then into fine tracheoles that reach individual cells. Gases move by diffusion, aided in active insects by abdominal pumping and by fluid withdrawal from tracheole ends, which increases the air-filled surface. The chitinous exoskeleton is impermeable, so gas exchange must be internal — a compromise between exchange and water conservation.","reforge":{"stem":"During flight, fluid is withdrawn from the ends of an insect's tracheoles. The effect is to:","options":{"A":"Reduce water loss through the spiracles to zero.","B":"Increase the volume of haemolymph reaching the flight muscles.","C":"Expose more surface for diffusion to active muscle","D":"Prevent carbon dioxide from leaving the tissues."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-05","stem":"The left ventricle has a thicker muscular wall than the right ventricle because it must:","options":{"A":"Hold a substantially greater volume of blood at each beat.","B":"Generate higher pressure to pump blood around the body","C":"Pump blood at a faster rate than the right side of the heart.","D":"Resist the higher pressure of blood returning from the lungs."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Both ventricles eject the same volume per beat; the difference is pressure. The right ventricle pumps to the lungs only — a short, low-resistance circuit where high pressure would damage capillaries and force fluid into alveoli. The left ventricle pumps into the aorta and around the entire systemic circuit, so it needs far more muscle. Atria have thin walls because they only push blood into the ventricles below them.","reforge":{"stem":"Why would a hole in the septum between the two ventricles reduce oxygen delivery to tissues?","options":{"A":"Blood would bypass the heart and enter the lungs directly.","B":"The atria would no longer contract before the ventricles.","C":"The semilunar valves would be unable to close during diastole.","D":"Oxygenated and deoxygenated blood would mix in the heart"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-06","stem":"The atrioventricular valves close during ventricular systole because:","options":{"A":"Nerve impulses from the sinoatrial node cause the valve cusps to contract.","B":"The atria relax and draw the valve cusps upwards.","C":"Ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure, forcing the cusps shut","D":"The semilunar valves open, creating suction in the atria above."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Valves are passive — they open and close according to pressure differences on either side, never by contracting. Cardiac cycle: atrial systole (atria contract, AV valves open, ventricles fill); ventricular systole (ventricular pressure exceeds atrial, AV valves shut giving the first heart sound, then exceeds arterial pressure so semilunar valves open); diastole (ventricles relax, pressure falls below arterial, semilunar valves shut giving the second sound). Tendinous cords stop the AV cusps inverting.","reforge":{"stem":"On a pressure-time graph of the cardiac cycle, the semilunar valves open at the moment when:","options":{"A":"Ventricular pressure rises above aortic pressure","B":"Atrial pressure rises above ventricular pressure.","C":"Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure.","D":"Aortic pressure reaches its minimum value."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-07","stem":"The purpose of the delay at the atrioventricular node is to ensure that:","options":{"A":"The heart rate is reduced during periods of rest.","B":"Blood in the coronary arteries is replenished between beats.","C":"The sinoatrial node can reset before the next impulse.","D":"The atria empty completely before the ventricles contract"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Conduction pathway: SAN (pacemaker in the right atrial wall) fires, the wave spreads across both atria causing atrial systole; the non-conducting fibrous tissue between atria and ventricles prevents direct spread; the AVN delays the impulse ~0.1s so atrial contraction finishes and ventricles fill fully; the impulse then travels down the bundle of His to the Purkyne fibres in the apex, so ventricles contract from the bottom up, pushing blood upwards into the arteries.","reforge":{"stem":"Why do the Purkyne fibres carry the impulse to the apex of the heart before the ventricles contract?","options":{"A":"So the atria and ventricles contract at exactly the same moment.","B":"So contraction begins at the base and forces blood upwards","C":"So the coronary arteries fill before the ventricles empty.","D":"So the semilunar valves close before ventricular systole begins."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-08","stem":"Arteries differ structurally from veins in that arteries have:","options":{"A":"Thick walls with more elastic tissue and smooth muscle","B":"A single layer of endothelium with no muscle or elastic tissue.","C":"Walls composed entirely of collagen with no elastic fibres.","D":"A wider lumen and numerous semilunar valves along their length."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Arteries: thick walls, much elastic tissue (stretch during systole and recoil during diastole, smoothing flow and maintaining pressure), smooth muscle for vasoconstriction, narrow lumen, no valves — pressure is high and flow is continuous. Veins: thin walls, wide lumen, little muscle or elastic tissue, valves to prevent backflow, and they rely on skeletal muscle contraction to return blood at low pressure. Capillaries: one cell thick endothelium for exchange.","reforge":{"stem":"Veins contain valves but arteries generally do not. This is because blood in veins:","options":{"A":"Is pumped directly by contraction of the vein walls.","B":"Contains no dissolved oxygen to drive its movement.","C":"Is at low pressure and would otherwise flow backwards","D":"Moves faster than blood in the corresponding arteries."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-N2-09","stem":"The oxygen dissociation curve for haemoglobin is S-shaped (sigmoid) because:","options":{"A":"Oxygen binds only at very high partial pressures in the tissues.","B":"Binding the first oxygen makes further binding easier","C":"Carbon dioxide competes with oxygen for the same binding site.","D":"Haemoglobin releases all four oxygen molecules simultaneously."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Haemoglobin has four haem groups. The first oxygen binds with difficulty (shallow initial slope), but binding changes the quaternary structure so the remaining sites bind more readily — cooperative binding, giving the steep middle section. Saturation then plateaus as sites fill. The steep region sits at tissue partial pressures, so a small fall in pO₂ causes a large release of oxygen — exactly where it is needed.","reforge":{"stem":"Actively respiring muscle produces carbon dioxide, shifting the dissociation curve to the right. This Bohr effect means haemoglobin:","options":{"A":"Binds oxygen more readily in the tissues than in the lungs.","B":"Carries carbon dioxide instead of oxygen in the plasma.","C":"Becomes permanently denatured by the lower pH.","D":"Releases more oxygen at a given partial pressure"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-N2-10","stem":"Fetal haemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult haemoglobin. This is necessary because:","options":{"A":"The fetus respires anaerobically throughout development.","B":"Fetal tissues require less oxygen than adult tissues do.","C":"Fetal blood must load oxygen at the placenta","D":"Fetal haemoglobin contains no haem groups to bind oxygen."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"At the placenta, maternal blood has already given up some oxygen, so the partial pressure is relatively low. Fetal haemoglobin's dissociation curve lies to the left of the adult curve — at any given pO₂ it is more saturated — so oxygen transfers from maternal to fetal blood down the gradient. The same principle explains why organisms in low-oxygen environments (llamas at altitude, lugworms in mud) have left-shifted curves.","reforge":{"stem":"A llama living at high altitude has a dissociation curve to the left of that of a lowland mammal. This adaptation allows it to:","options":{"A":"Load oxygen despite the low partial pressure in air","B":"Reduce the total amount of haemoglobin needed in the blood.","C":"Release oxygen more readily to its respiring tissues.","D":"Prevent carbon dioxide from binding to haemoglobin at altitude."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-N2-11","stem":"Tissue fluid is formed at the arterial end of a capillary bed mainly because:","options":{"A":"The water potential of the plasma is higher than that of the tissue.","B":"Plasma proteins are actively pumped out of the capillary.","C":"Red blood cells squeeze through gaps in the endothelium.","D":"Hydrostatic pressure exceeds the opposing oncotic pressure"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"At the arterial end, high hydrostatic (blood) pressure forces water and small solutes out through gaps in the capillary endothelium; plasma proteins are too large to leave, so they stay and lower the water potential of the blood. At the venous end, hydrostatic pressure has fallen while the oncotic (osmotic) pull of the retained proteins is unchanged, so most fluid returns by osmosis. The remaining ~10% drains into lymph capillaries and returns via the thoracic duct.","reforge":{"stem":"Severe protein deficiency causes oedema — accumulation of tissue fluid. This is because low plasma protein concentration:","options":{"A":"Increases hydrostatic pressure at the arterial end of capillaries.","B":"Raises blood water potential, so less fluid returns by osmosis","C":"Blocks the lymphatic vessels draining the tissue.","D":"Prevents red blood cells from entering the capillary bed."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-N2-12","stem":"Xylem vessels are adapted for water transport because they are:","options":{"A":"Dead, hollow tubes with lignified walls and no end walls","B":"Composed of sieve tubes connected by perforated end plates.","C":"Living cells with dense cytoplasm and many mitochondria.","D":"Surrounded by companion cells that supply them with ATP."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Xylem: dead at maturity, no cytoplasm or end walls, forming a continuous hollow tube; lignified walls give strength and waterproofing and prevent collapse under tension; pits allow lateral movement. Transports water and mineral ions upwards only, passively. Phloem: living sieve tube elements with perforated sieve plates, supported by companion cells with many mitochondria; transports assimilates (mainly sucrose) both up and down, requiring ATP.","reforge":{"stem":"A ring of bark including the phloem is removed from a tree trunk. Sugars accumulate above the ring because:","options":{"A":"Xylem transport of water has been interrupted at that point.","B":"Lignin from the bark blocks the vessels below the ring.","C":"Translocation of sucrose down the stem has been prevented","D":"Root pressure forces sucrose upwards past the cut."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.3"},{"id":"BIO-N2-13","stem":"Transpiration rate would be expected to increase most when:","options":{"A":"Humidity rises and air movement around the leaf falls.","B":"Air movement increases and humidity falls","C":"Light intensity falls, causing stomata to close.","D":"Temperature falls, reducing the kinetic energy of water molecules."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Transpiration is evaporation from mesophyll cell walls followed by diffusion of vapour out through stomata. Increased by: higher light (stomata open), higher temperature (more kinetic energy, steeper gradient), lower humidity (steeper water potential gradient), more air movement (removes the humid boundary layer). Decreased by high humidity, still air and darkness. A potometer actually measures water uptake, which approximates transpiration since most uptake is lost as vapour.","reforge":{"stem":"A potometer measures water uptake rather than transpiration directly. This is an acceptable approximation because:","options":{"A":"Water uptake and transpiration are entirely unrelated processes.","B":"Photosynthesis consumes most of the water that is absorbed.","C":"The plant stores all absorbed water in the xylem vessels.","D":"Around 99% of water taken up is lost as vapour"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.3"},{"id":"BIO-N2-14","stem":"Xerophytes such as marram grass reduce water loss by:","options":{"A":"Increasing stomatal density on the upper leaf surface.","B":"Removing the waxy cuticle to allow evaporative cooling.","C":"Rolling leaves and sinking stomata into pits","D":"Growing broad, thin leaves with a large surface area."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Xerophytic adaptations all reduce the water potential gradient or the area for evaporation: thick waxy cuticle; stomata sunken in pits and surrounded by hairs, trapping humid air so the gradient is shallow; rolled leaves enclosing a humid chamber; reduced leaves or spines (cacti) minimising surface area; stomata opening at night (CAM). Hydrophytes show the opposite — stomata on the upper surface, air spaces for buoyancy.","reforge":{"stem":"Cacti have spines instead of broad leaves and carry out photosynthesis in the stem. The main advantage is:","options":{"A":"A greatly reduced surface area for water loss by transpiration","B":"An increased number of stomata for gas exchange.","C":"A higher rate of water uptake through the spines.","D":"A thinner cuticle allowing more light to reach the chloroplasts."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-15","stem":"According to the mass flow hypothesis, sucrose is loaded into phloem at the source by:","options":{"A":"Simple diffusion through the phospholipid bilayer of sieve tubes.","B":"Osmosis driven by the higher water potential of the sieve tube.","C":"Passive uptake through open sieve plate pores from the xylem.","D":"Active transport, using companion cells and a proton gradient"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Loading at the source: companion cells actively pump H⁺ out using ATP, then H⁺ re-enters via a cotransporter bringing sucrose with it; sucrose passes into the sieve tube. This lowers the water potential there, so water enters from the xylem by osmosis, raising hydrostatic pressure. At the sink, sucrose is removed and used or stored, water leaves, and pressure falls. The resulting pressure gradient drives mass flow from source to sink.","reforge":{"stem":"Which observation provides evidence supporting the mass flow hypothesis?","options":{"A":"Xylem vessels are dead and lignified at maturity.","B":"Sap exudes from a cut aphid stylet, showing phloem pressure","C":"Transpiration continues at night when stomata are closed.","D":"Sucrose is found in equal concentrations at source and sink."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.3"},{"id":"BIO-N2-16","stem":"Guard cells open the stomatal pore when they:","options":{"A":"Take up potassium ions and water, becoming turgid","B":"Lose water to neighbouring epidermal cells and become flaccid.","C":"Actively transport carbon dioxide into the leaf air spaces.","D":"Increase the thickness of the cuticle on the outer wall."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Guard cells have unevenly thickened walls — the inner wall bordering the pore is thicker and less elastic. Active uptake of K⁺ lowers water potential, water enters by osmosis, and the cells become turgid; because the outer wall stretches more than the inner, the pair bows apart and the pore opens. Water loss reverses this and the pore closes. Abscisic acid released in drought triggers closure even in light.","reforge":{"stem":"During drought, abscisic acid causes stomata to close. The immediate benefit to the plant is:","options":{"A":"An increased rate of carbon dioxide uptake for photosynthesis.","B":"A greater rate of water uptake by the root hair cells.","C":"Reduced water loss by transpiration through the stomatal pores","D":"Faster translocation of sucrose from source to sink."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-17","stem":"Root hair cells are adapted for absorbing mineral ions because they have:","options":{"A":"A thick waxy cuticle preventing solute loss to the soil.","B":"A large surface area and many mitochondria for uptake","C":"Chloroplasts for producing glucose used in ion uptake.","D":"Lignified walls that resist the entry of water by osmosis."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Root hairs are long extensions of epidermal cells, giving a large surface area in contact with soil water. Mineral ions are usually more concentrated inside the cell than in soil water, so uptake is by active transport — hence the many mitochondria supplying ATP. Taking up ions lowers the cell's water potential, so water then follows by osmosis. Water crosses the cortex by the apoplast, symplast and vacuolar pathways.","reforge":{"stem":"Water crossing the root cortex must pass through the symplast pathway at the endodermis because:","options":{"A":"The apoplast route carries water faster than the cells can absorb it.","B":"Root hair cells have no cytoplasmic connections to the cortex.","C":"Xylem vessels are living and require cytoplasmic contact.","D":"The waterproof Casparian strip blocks the apoplast route"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.3"},{"id":"BIO-N2-18","stem":"A single circulatory system is less efficient for large active mammals than a double system because in a single system blood:","options":{"A":"Cannot carry oxygen bound to a respiratory pigment.","B":"Passes through the heart twice for every complete circuit.","C":"Loses pressure passing through the gas exchange capillaries","D":"Travels only to the respiratory surface and never to the tissues."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Single circulation (fish): blood passes through the heart once per circuit — heart → gills → body → heart. Pressure drops substantially crossing the narrow gill capillaries, so blood reaches the body slowly. Double circulation (mammals, birds): pulmonary and systemic circuits, blood returns to the heart to be repressurised before going to the body, allowing a fast delivery rate and high metabolic rate. Insects have an open system with haemolymph in a haemocoel.","reforge":{"stem":"In an open circulatory system such as an insect's, transport is less precisely controlled because haemolymph:","options":{"A":"Bathes tissues directly rather than staying in vessels","B":"Carries oxygen bound to haemoglobin in red cells.","C":"Is pumped at very high pressure through closed vessels.","D":"Passes through the heart twice in every circuit of the body."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-N2-19","stem":"Atheroma formation in a coronary artery increases the risk of myocardial infarction because it:","options":{"A":"Increases the elasticity of the artery wall during systole.","B":"Prevents the atrioventricular valves from closing fully.","C":"Raises the oxygen concentration of blood in the coronary vessels.","D":"Narrows the lumen, reducing blood flow to cardiac muscle"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Sequence: damage to the endothelium (from high blood pressure, smoking, high LDL) → inflammatory response → cholesterol and white cells accumulate forming a plaque (atheroma) → lumen narrows and the wall stiffens → blood flow falls. If a plaque ruptures, a clot (thrombus) forms and may block the artery completely. In a coronary artery this starves cardiac muscle of oxygen, causing a myocardial infarction; in a cerebral artery it causes a stroke.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does an aneurysm most often develop at a site already weakened by atheroma?","options":{"A":"Blood flow through the artery becomes faster and smoother.","B":"The weakened wall stretches and bulges under arterial pressure","C":"The artery wall gains additional elastic fibres at that point.","D":"Cholesterol dissolves the lignin in the vessel wall."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-N2-20","stem":"Which sequence correctly traces a red blood cell from the vena cava to the aorta?","options":{"A":"Right atrium → right ventricle → lungs → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta","B":"Left atrium → left ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs → aorta.","C":"Right atrium → left ventricle → pulmonary vein → lungs → aorta.","D":"Right ventricle → right atrium → lungs → left ventricle → left atrium → aorta."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Deoxygenated blood: vena cava → right atrium → tricuspid valve → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs. Oxygenated blood: pulmonary vein → left atrium → bicuspid valve → left ventricle → aorta → body. Two rules resolve most confusion: arteries always carry blood away from the heart (so the pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood), and blood always passes atrium → ventricle, never the reverse.","reforge":{"stem":"The pulmonary artery is unusual among arteries because it:","options":{"A":"Contains valves along its entire length.","B":"Carries blood towards the heart from the lungs.","C":"Carries deoxygenated blood away from the heart","D":"Has a thinner wall than the corresponding vein."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-N2-21","stem":"Starch is digested to glucose by the combined action of:","options":{"A":"Endopeptidases and exopeptidases in the stomach lumen.","B":"Amylase and then maltase in the small intestine","C":"Lipase and bile salts in the duodenum.","D":"Trypsin secreted by the pancreas and activated by enterokinase."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Carbohydrate digestion: salivary and pancreatic amylase hydrolyse starch to maltose; membrane-bound disaccharidases in the ileum epithelium (maltase, sucrase, lactase) complete the job to monosaccharides. Protein: endopeptidases cut internal bonds creating more ends, exopeptidases remove terminal amino acids, dipeptidases finish. Lipid: bile salts emulsify into droplets increasing surface area, then lipase hydrolyses to fatty acids and monoglycerides which form micelles.","reforge":{"stem":"Bile salts contain no enzymes, yet they increase the rate of lipid digestion because they:","options":{"A":"Hydrolyse ester bonds in triglyceride molecules directly.","B":"Neutralise pancreatic lipase before it reaches the ileum.","C":"Transport fatty acids across the epithelial cell membrane.","D":"Emulsify lipids into droplets, raising surface area"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-22","stem":"Glucose is absorbed from the ileum lumen into epithelial cells against its concentration gradient by:","options":{"A":"Simple diffusion through the phospholipid bilayer.","B":"Osmosis following the movement of water molecules.","C":"Co-transport with sodium ions, driven by ATP","D":"Exocytosis of vesicles containing dissolved glucose."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-MEMBRANE-TRANSPORT","scaffold":"Co-transport in the ileum: the sodium-potassium pump on the basal membrane actively removes Na⁺ from the epithelial cell using ATP, creating a low internal Na⁺ concentration. Na⁺ then diffuses in from the lumen through a co-transporter protein, carrying glucose with it — even against the glucose gradient. Glucose then leaves into the blood by facilitated diffusion. The ATP is used indirectly, which is why this is called secondary active transport.","reforge":{"stem":"Villi and microvilli increase absorption in the ileum principally by:","options":{"A":"Greatly increasing the surface area for absorption","B":"Reducing the distance between the lumen and the lacteal.","C":"Actively pumping nutrients into the hepatic portal vein.","D":"Producing additional digestive enzymes in the lumen."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-23","stem":"Cholera toxin causes severe diarrhoea because it:","options":{"A":"Destroys the villi, preventing all nutrient absorption.","B":"Blocks the sodium-potassium pump on the basal membrane.","C":"Digests the phospholipid bilayer of epithelial cell membranes.","D":"Opens chloride channels, so ions and water leave cells"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Vibrio cholerae toxin permanently opens CFTR chloride channels in the intestinal epithelium. Cl⁻ floods into the lumen, Na⁺ follows to balance charge, and the lumen's water potential falls sharply, so water leaves the cells and blood by osmosis — producing watery diarrhoea and dangerous dehydration. Oral rehydration solutions contain both glucose and sodium precisely because the co-transporter still works and drags water back in with them.","reforge":{"stem":"Oral rehydration solutions contain glucose as well as salts. The glucose is included because it:","options":{"A":"Provides the energy needed to kill the cholera bacteria.","B":"Allows sodium co-transport, so water is reabsorbed","C":"Lowers the water potential of the intestinal lumen further.","D":"Neutralises the toxin released by the bacteria."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-24","stem":"Haemoglobin transports most carbon dioxide from respiring tissues in the form of:","options":{"A":"Hydrogencarbonate ions dissolved in the blood plasma","B":"Carbaminohaemoglobin within the red blood cells.","C":"Carbon dioxide gas dissolved directly in the plasma.","D":"Carbonic anhydrase bound to the red cell membrane."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"CO₂ transport: about 85% as hydrogencarbonate ions, ~10% as carbaminohaemoglobin, ~5% dissolved in plasma. In the red cell, carbonic anhydrase catalyses CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃, which dissociates into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻. HCO₃⁻ diffuses into the plasma and Cl⁻ moves in to maintain charge balance (the chloride shift). The H⁺ is buffered by haemoglobin forming haemoglobinic acid, and this fall in pH is what causes the Bohr shift.","reforge":{"stem":"The chloride shift refers to the movement of chloride ions:","options":{"A":"Out of the red blood cell as hydrogencarbonate enters it.","B":"Across the alveolar epithelium into the air in the lungs.","C":"From the plasma into the red cell to balance charge","D":"From haemoglobin into the plasma during oxygen loading."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-N2-25","stem":"A student calculates cardiac output. The correct relationship is:","options":{"A":"Cardiac output = stroke volume ÷ heart rate.","B":"Cardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate","C":"Cardiac output = stroke volume + heart rate.","D":"Cardiac output = heart rate ÷ stroke volume."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Cardiac output (cm³ min⁻¹) = stroke volume (cm³ per beat) × heart rate (beats min⁻¹). Typical resting values: 70cm³ × 70 bpm ≈ 4900cm³ min⁻¹. During exercise both terms rise, so output can reach 20-30 dm³ min⁻¹. Trained athletes have a larger stroke volume, so their resting heart rate is lower for the same output — this is why bradycardia in an athlete is a sign of fitness rather than illness.","reforge":{"stem":"An athlete has a stroke volume of 100cm³ and a resting heart rate of 50 bpm. Their cardiac output is:","options":{"A":"150 cm³ min⁻¹, found by adding the two values.","B":"2 cm³ min⁻¹, found by dividing 100 by 50.","C":"0.5 cm³ min⁻¹, found by dividing 50 by 100.","D":"5000 cm³ min⁻¹, found by multiplying 100 by 50"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-26","stem":"Lymph differs from tissue fluid mainly in that lymph:","options":{"A":"Is pumped directly by the left ventricle of the heart.","B":"Contains red blood cells and plasma proteins in high concentration.","C":"Contains lymphocytes and more fatty acids, draining to veins","D":"Is formed at the arterial end of every capillary bed."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Tissue fluid forms from plasma at the arterial end of capillaries; most returns at the venous end. The ~10% that does not enters blind-ended lymph capillaries as lymph. Lymph is similar to tissue fluid but contains more lymphocytes (added at lymph nodes) and, after a meal, many fatty acids absorbed via the lacteals of the villi. It moves by skeletal muscle compression with valves preventing backflow, returning to the blood at the subclavian veins.","reforge":{"stem":"Lymph vessels contain valves and lie between skeletal muscles. This arrangement is necessary because lymph:","options":{"A":"Has no pump and relies on body movement to flow","B":"Is under high pressure from ventricular contraction.","C":"Must flow in both directions to reach the lymph nodes.","D":"Contains red blood cells that would otherwise settle out."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-27","stem":"Surface area to volume ratio explains why large organisms need specialised exchange surfaces. As an organism gets larger, its:","options":{"A":"Surface area increases faster than its volume does.","B":"Volume and surface area increase at exactly the same rate.","C":"Metabolic rate per unit mass rises, requiring less oxygen overall.","D":"Surface area to volume ratio falls, so diffusion is too slow"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Surface area scales with length², volume with length³, so the ratio falls as size increases. A single-celled organism has a ratio high enough for diffusion across the body surface to supply every part. Larger organisms have a small ratio and a longer diffusion pathway to the centre, so they need specialised exchange surfaces (alveoli, gills, villi) plus a mass transport system to carry substances between the exchange surface and the tissues.","reforge":{"stem":"A small mammal loses heat faster per gram of body mass than a large mammal. This is because the small mammal has:","options":{"A":"A lower metabolic rate per unit of body mass.","B":"A larger surface area relative to its volume","C":"A thicker layer of insulating fat beneath the skin.","D":"A smaller total surface area across its whole body."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"},{"id":"BIO-N2-28","stem":"Which feature of capillaries most directly enables efficient exchange with tissues?","options":{"A":"Walls one cell thick, giving a short diffusion distance","B":"A thick layer of smooth muscle allowing vasoconstriction.","C":"Valves at intervals preventing the backflow of blood.","D":"A wide lumen allowing rapid, high-pressure blood flow."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Capillary adaptations: endothelium one cell thick so the diffusion pathway is minimal; a lumen roughly the diameter of a red blood cell, which slows flow and forces cells against the wall, increasing time and closeness for exchange; gaps (fenestrations) between endothelial cells letting plasma and small solutes leave; extensive branching giving a large total surface area and ensuring no cell is far from a capillary.","reforge":{"stem":"Blood flows more slowly through capillaries than through arteries. This is beneficial because it:","options":{"A":"Reduces the total surface area available for exchange.","B":"Prevents plasma proteins from leaving the capillary.","C":"Allows more time for diffusion into the tissues","D":"Increases the hydrostatic pressure at the venous end."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-2","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.1"}]},"BIO-3":{"label":"Genetics, Inheritance & Evolution","color":"#14532d","questions":[{"id":"GEN-01","stem":"A student states that all of an organism's DNA codes for proteins. Which statement correctly identifies the error?","options":{"A":"Most DNA is non-coding: introns, regulatory and repetitive sequences","B":"All DNA codes for proteins — the student is correct.","C":"Only DNA in the nucleus codes for proteins; mitochondrial DNA does not.","D":"DNA codes for RNA only, never proteins directly."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"Coding DNA (exons): sequences transcribed and translated into protein. Non-coding DNA: introns (spliced out post-transcription), regulatory sequences (promoters, enhancers), repetitive sequences (satellite DNA, telomeres). In humans, only ~2% of DNA codes for protein. Exam trap: confusing 'non-coding' with 'non-functional' — regulatory sequences are non-coding but essential. Also: all DNA is transcribed into pre-mRNA, but introns are removed before translation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can non-coding DNA still have a critical function?","options":{"A":"It codes for structural proteins not detected by standard analysis.","B":"Non-coding DNA protects coding DNA from mutation.","C":"Non-coding promoters and enhancers regulate gene expression","D":"It stores energy for the cell during transcription."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.2"},{"id":"GEN-02","stem":"In meiosis, which process generates genetic variation by exchanging segments between homologous chromosomes?","options":{"A":"Semi-conservative replication","B":"Crossing over (chiasmata formation) during prophase I","C":"Telophase, because nuclear envelopes could not re-form.","D":"Cytokinesis producing haploid daughter cells"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-DIVISION","scaffold":"Two main sources of genetic variation in meiosis: (1) Crossing over (prophase I): homologous chromosomes pair up (bivalents), non-sister chromatids exchange segments at chiasmata — produces new allele combinations on chromosomes; (2) Independent assortment (metaphase I): maternal and paternal homologues align randomly → 2²³ possible combinations in humans. Distinguish from semi-conservative replication (DNA replication, not meiosis) and cytokinesis (cell division, not recombination).","reforge":{"stem":"A student says independent assortment produces more genetic variation than crossing over. Is this correct?","options":{"A":"Yes — independent assortment is more important because it affects all chromosomes at once.","B":"No — crossing over does not actually occur in humans during meiosis.","C":"Yes — crossing over moves one segment while independent assortment randomises all chromosomes.","D":"Both matter: assortment gives 2²³ combinations, crossing over adds more"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.2"},{"id":"GEN-03","stem":"A woman is a carrier of a recessive X-linked disorder. Her partner is unaffected. What is the probability that their son will be affected?","options":{"A":"0% — sons cannot inherit X-linked disorders from carriers.","B":"25% of all children","C":"50% of sons","D":"100% of sons"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"X-linked recessive: carrier mother = X^A X^a (one normal, one recessive allele). Father = X^A Y. Sons receive Y from father, X from mother. Mother passes X^A or X^a with equal probability (50%). A son who receives X^a is affected (no second X to mask it). So 50% of sons affected. 50% of daughters are carriers. Overall: 25% of all children affected (all are sons). Exam trap: stating 25% of all children, not 50% of sons.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are males more likely to express X-linked recessive conditions than females?","options":{"A":"Males have one X, so a single recessive allele is expressed","B":"Males have weaker immune systems, making X-linked conditions more likely to develop.","C":"The Y chromosome actively promotes expression of recessive alleles on the X chromosome.","D":"X-linked alleles mutate more frequently in males due to higher cell division rates."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.2"},{"id":"GEN-04","stem":"Natural selection acts on phenotype, not genotype. Which statement best explains why this distinction matters?","options":{"A":"It means that only visible characteristics can evolve.","B":"It is irrelevant — phenotype and genotype always correspond directly. The biological explanation must account for the cells, molecules, structures, mechanisms and conditions stated; the answer treats an exception or consequence as though it were the underlying principle.","C":"It means that environmental adaptations cannot be inherited.","D":"Carriers of recessive alleles (Aa) have the same phenotype as dominant homozygotes (AA) but different genotypes — selection cannot distinguish them, so recessive alleles persist in the population even when they reduce fitness in homozygous form."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"Selection acts on phenotype — what the organism looks like and how it functions. Carriers (Aa) are phenotypically normal but carry one copy of a harmful recessive allele. Selection cannot eliminate the recessive allele from carriers. This explains why harmful recessive alleles (e.g. cystic fibrosis allele) persist at low frequency in populations — they are 'hidden' in heterozygous carriers and only exposed to selection in homozygous recessives (aa).","reforge":{"stem":"A harmful recessive allele causes severe disease in homozygotes but not carriers. Why can natural selection not eliminate this allele from the population?","options":{"A":"Natural selection can only act on dominant alleles — recessive ones are invisible to it.","B":"Heterozygous carriers are unaffected, so the allele escapes selection","C":"The allele mutates back to the dominant form faster than selection can remove it.","D":"Harmful alleles are always dominant and therefore always visible to selection."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-4.3"},{"id":"GEN-05","stem":"The Hardy-Weinberg principle states that allele frequencies in a population will remain constant IF certain conditions are met. Which is NOT one of these conditions?","options":{"A":"Organisms reproduce sexually","B":"Random mating","C":"No natural selection, mutation, genetic drift, or gene flow","D":"Large population size"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"Hardy-Weinberg conditions: (1) Large population (prevents genetic drift); (2) Random mating (no sexual selection); (3) No natural selection; (4) No mutation; (5) No gene flow (migration). Sexual reproduction is assumed — the principle applies to sexually reproducing diploid organisms, so 'organisms reproduce sexually' is a background assumption, not a condition for equilibrium maintenance. Exam trap: listing sexual reproduction as a condition rather than a prerequisite.","reforge":{"stem":"A population of rare mountain goats is split by a new road. Over generations, the two populations diverge genetically. Which Hardy-Weinberg condition is being violated?","options":{"A":"No mutation — the road causes DNA mutations.","B":"No natural selection — the road selects for different traits.","C":"No gene flow, so allele frequencies diverge","D":"Random mating — goats can no longer choose mates freely."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.2"},{"id":"GEN-06","stem":"What does the difference between discontinuous and continuous variation look like?","options":{"A":"Discontinuous variation is always caused by genes; continuous variation is always caused by the environment.","B":"Discontinuous variation gives distinct categories; continuous gives a range forming a normal distribution","C":"Continuous variation only occurs in plants; discontinuous variation only in animals.","D":"Discontinuous variation is more common in human populations than continuous variation."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CELL-DIVISION","scaffold":"Discontinuous: controlled by one or few genes (monogenic), phenotypes fall into distinct groups, little environmental influence. Examples: ABO blood group, tongue rolling, dimples. Continuous: controlled by many genes (polygenic) + environmental influence. Normal distribution. Examples: height, weight, skin colour, intelligence. The distinction is not purely genetic vs environmental — discontinuous traits can also be influenced by environment, but the key feature is the presence or absence of intermediates.","reforge":{"stem":"Height in humans shows continuous variation. Why does this produce a normal distribution rather than distinct categories?","options":{"A":"Height is determined entirely by diet and environment — genes play no role.","B":"A normal distribution only appears when environment has absolutely no effect on the trait.","C":"Height varies continuously because humans keep growing throughout their adult lives.","D":"Many genes add small effects, and environment broadens the range"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.2"},{"id":"GEN-07","stem":"During transcription, which strand of DNA serves as the template strand?","options":{"A":"Individual bacteria 'learn' to resist the antibiotic and pass this acquired resistance to offspring.","B":"Both strands are transcribed simultaneously into the same mRNA.","C":"The template (antisense) strand — RNA polymerase reads it 3′ to 5′ and synthesises mRNA 5′ to 3′.","D":"Either strand, depending on which gene is being expressed."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-DNA-PROTEIN","scaffold":"Transcription: RNA polymerase binds at the promoter and reads the template (antisense) strand in the 3′→5′ direction, synthesising mRNA in the 5′→3′ direction. The mRNA sequence matches the coding (sense) strand (with U replacing T). Exam trap: students confuse template with coding strand. Template = complementary to mRNA; coding = same sequence as mRNA (except T→U). Different genes on the same chromosome may use different strands as template.","reforge":{"stem":"The coding strand of a gene reads 5′-ATGGCCTAG-3′. What is the mRNA sequence produced from this gene?","options":{"A":"5′-AUGGCCUAG-3′","B":"5′-ATGGCCTAG-3′","C":"3′-TACCGGATC-5′","D":"3′-UACCGGAUC-5′"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"GEN-08","stem":"A population of bacteria is exposed to an antibiotic. After several generations, most bacteria are resistant. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"Individual bacteria 'learn' to resist the antibiotic and pass this acquired resistance to offspring.","B":"The antibiotic causes mutations in bacterial DNA that create resistance.","C":"All bacteria develop resistance simultaneously through phenotypic plasticity.","D":"Resistance alleles already existed; the antibiotic killed susceptible bacteria"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Natural selection, NOT Lamarckian inheritance: (1) Variation existed before antibiotic exposure — some bacteria had random mutations conferring resistance; (2) Antibiotic = selection pressure; (3) Susceptible bacteria die; (4) Resistant bacteria survive and reproduce; (5) Resistance allele frequency increases over generations. The antibiotic does not cause resistance — it selects for pre-existing resistance. Key exam distinction: selection acts on existing variation; it does not direct mutation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is it wrong to say 'the antibiotic caused the bacteria to become resistant'?","options":{"A":"Bacteria cannot change their genetic code, so antibiotic resistance cannot develop.","B":"The antibiotic selected pre-existing resistant variants rather than causing them","C":"Antibiotics are not mutagenic chemicals, so they cannot cause resistance mutations.","D":"Resistance developed too rapidly for antibiotic exposure to be the cause of the mutation."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.6"},{"id":"BIO-E3-01","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"A pathogen is:","options":{"A":"an organism or agent that causes disease","B":"Osmotic entry of the bacterium down a water potential gradient.","C":"a type of allele","D":"a non-living nutrient"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Pathogens include bacteria, viruses, fungi and protists that cause disease.","reforge":{"stem":"A student catches influenza after coming into contact with someone carrying the virus. What term describes the virus responsible for causing this illness?","options":{"A":"A pathogen, an organism or agent that causes disease","B":"A beneficial antibody in a typical cell in this biological system","C":"A type of allele","D":"A non-living nutrient"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-4.1"},{"id":"BIO-E3-02","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"Natural selection requires that individuals in a population:","options":{"A":"are genetically identical","B":"vary and differ in reproductive success","C":"The antibiotic causes mutations in bacterial DNA that create resistance.","D":"avoid competition completely"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Heritable variation and differential survival or reproduction change allele frequencies.","reforge":{"stem":"Within a population of beetles, some individuals happen to survive a new predator better than others because of small differences between them, and those survivors reproduce more successfully. What condition does natural selection require to act on a population like this?","options":{"A":"All individuals must be genetically identical","B":"Individuals must vary and differ in reproductive success","C":"Every individual must acquire the same mutation in a typical cell","D":"Individuals must avoid competition completely"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.6"},{"id":"BIO-E3-03","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"Biodiversity describes:","options":{"A":"the number of cells in one organism","B":"only the number of endangered mammals","C":"the variety of life within an area","D":"the rate of photosynthesis"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Biodiversity includes genetic, species and ecosystem diversity.","reforge":{"stem":"An ecologist compares a monoculture field with an ancient woodland and counts a far greater number of different species living in the woodland. What is she measuring?","options":{"A":"The number of cells in one organism","B":"Only the number of endangered mammals present in a typical cell","C":"Biodiversity, the variety of life within an area","D":"The rate of photosynthesis occurring"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-4.2"},{"id":"BIO-E3-04","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"A classification system is useful because it:","options":{"A":"makes every organism identical","B":"Resistance developed too rapidly for antibiotic exposure to be the cause of the mutation.","C":"measures population growth only","D":"organises organisms according to shared characteristics and evolutionary relationships"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Modern classification aims to reflect evolutionary relationships using evidence.","reforge":{"stem":"Biologists group organisms into a hierarchy of kingdoms, phyla, classes and so on, based on shared features and evolutionary history. Why is this classification system useful?","options":{"A":"It makes every organism identical","B":"It removes the need for identification in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology","C":"It measures population growth only","D":"It organises organisms according to shared characteristics and evolutionary relationships"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-4.3"},{"id":"BIO-TRANSPORT-01","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"Why are red blood cells efficient oxygen carriers?","options":{"A":"They have no nucleus and contain haemoglobin","B":"A lymph vessel, carrying fluid to the right atrium","C":"They contain many chloroplasts","D":"They pump blood by contraction"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Their biconcave shape and lack of a nucleus provide space and surface area for haemoglobin.","reforge":{"stem":"A red blood cell has no nucleus and is packed almost entirely with a single protein. Why does this structure make it an efficient oxygen carrier?","options":{"A":"The lack of a nucleus and the presence of haemoglobin maximise oxygen-carrying capacity","B":"It has thick cellulose walls","C":"It contains many chloroplasts in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology","D":"It pumps blood by contracting"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-TRANSPORT-02","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"At the start of a capillary bed, hydrostatic pressure is high because:","options":{"A":"The pulmonary artery, carrying blood from the lungs to the aorta","B":"blood has just left an arteriole under pressure","C":"lymph is entering the artery","D":"red cells are producing plasma"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Pressure is greatest near the arteriole end, promoting formation of tissue fluid.","reforge":{"stem":"At the arteriole end of a capillary bed, the hydrostatic pressure pushing fluid out is at its highest. Why is the pressure so high at this particular point?","options":{"A":"The venule has stopped all filtration in a typical cell","B":"Blood has just left an arteriole under pressure","C":"Lymph is entering the artery","D":"Red blood cells are producing plasma"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-TRANSPORT-03","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"How does the lymphatic system support blood circulation?","options":{"A":"All chambers contracting permanently at once","B":"It produces bile for digestion","C":"It returns excess tissue fluid to the blood","D":"It carries impulses to the heart"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Lymph vessels return fluid that has left capillaries and help prevent tissue swelling.","reforge":{"stem":"Not all of the tissue fluid that leaves capillaries returns to them directly; some is collected by a separate network of vessels. How does this network support blood circulation?","options":{"A":"It oxygenates blood in the alveoli","B":"It produces bile for digestion","C":"It returns excess tissue fluid to the blood","D":"It carries electrical impulses to the heart"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-TRANSPORT-04","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"Why do arteries have thick muscular walls?","options":{"A":"They allow valves to open at every branch","B":"They provide the main site of diffusion","C":"They contain air spaces for ventilation","D":"They withstand and maintain high pressure"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Elastic and muscular tissue helps arteries tolerate pressure and smooth the pulse.","reforge":{"stem":"Arteries carrying blood away from the heart have noticeably thicker, more muscular walls than veins. What is the main reason for this structural difference?","options":{"A":"They allow valves to open at every branch in a typical cell","B":"They provide the main site of diffusion","C":"They contain air spaces for ventilation","D":"They must withstand and maintain high pressure"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-TRANSPORT-05","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"The pressure in the left ventricle is greater than in the right ventricle because:","options":{"A":"it must drive blood around the systemic circuit","B":"it pumps blood only to the lungs","C":"It mixes oxygenated and deoxygenated blood completely in a typical cell","D":"it has no atrioventricular valve"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"The systemic circuit has greater resistance and distance than the pulmonary circuit.","reforge":{"stem":"The wall of the heart's left ventricle is noticeably thicker and more muscular than that of the right ventricle. Why does the left ventricle need to generate greater pressure?","options":{"A":"It must drive blood all the way around the systemic circuit","B":"It pumps blood only to the lungs","C":"It receives blood directly from the vena cava in a typical cell","D":"It has no atrioventricular valve"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-TRANSPORT-06","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"What is the main benefit of a double circulatory system?","options":{"A":"A thick, muscular wall that actively pumps oxygen into the blood.","B":"It separates pulmonary and systemic pressure circuits","C":"It makes the heart unnecessary","D":"It carries only deoxygenated blood"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Separate circuits allow suitable pressure for lung exchange and efficient delivery to tissues.","reforge":{"stem":"A mammal's heart pumps blood through two separate circuits, one to the lungs and one to the rest of the body, rather than a single loop. What is the main benefit of keeping these two circuits separate?","options":{"A":"It prevents blood from entering capillaries in a typical cell","B":"It separates pulmonary and systemic pressure circuits","C":"It makes the heart unnecessary","D":"It carries only deoxygenated blood"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-TRANSPORT-07","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"Why is tissue fluid formation greater at the arteriole end of a capillary?","options":{"A":"A dry surface that prevents gases from dissolving before diffusion.","B":"Osmosis cannot occur near venules","C":"Hydrostatic pressure exceeds the opposing water potential","D":"The lymph vessels close permanently"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"A net outward pressure pushes fluid through the capillary wall at the arteriole end.","reforge":{"stem":"More tissue fluid forms at the arteriole end of a capillary bed than at the venule end. Why does more fluid leave the capillary at this end specifically?","options":{"A":"Plasma proteins leave the blood at that end","B":"Osmosis cannot occur near venules","C":"Hydrostatic pressure there exceeds the opposing water potential","D":"The lymph vessels close permanently at that end in a typical cell"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-TRANSPORT-08","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"What happens to most tissue fluid at the venule end?","options":{"A":"It becomes red blood cells","B":"A store of haemoglobin held within the alveolar epithelial cells.","C":"It is converted directly into glucose","D":"It returns to capillaries as hydrostatic pressure falls"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Lower hydrostatic pressure and the water potential gradient favour fluid re-entry.","reforge":{"stem":"By the time blood reaches the venule end of a capillary bed, the hydrostatic pressure has fallen considerably. What happens to most of the tissue fluid at this point?","options":{"A":"It becomes red blood cells","B":"It enters the alveoli for gas exchange in a typical cell in this biological system","C":"It is converted directly into glucose","D":"It returns to the capillaries as hydrostatic pressure falls"},"correct":"D"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-TRANSPORT-09","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"Why does haemoglobin become saturated in the lungs?","options":{"A":"The high oxygen partial pressure favours oxygen loading","B":"Carbon dioxide blocks every haem group","C":"The blood has no plasma proteins","D":"Contract, decreasing thoracic volume and raising internal pressure."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"High oxygen partial pressure in the lungs promotes formation of oxyhaemoglobin.","reforge":{"stem":"Blood passing through the lungs becomes almost fully saturated with oxygen before it returns to the heart. What makes haemoglobin bind oxygen so readily at this point?","options":{"A":"The high oxygen partial pressure in the lungs favours oxygen loading","B":"Carbon dioxide blocks every haem group","C":"The blood has no plasma proteins","D":"The alveoli contain no concentration gradient in a typical cell in this biological system"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-3.2"},{"id":"BIO-TRANSPORT-10","spec":"BIO-3","stem":"During exercise, oxygen unloading in active muscle increases because:","options":{"A":"muscle temperature always falls","B":"lower oxygen partial pressure and higher carbon dioxide reduce haemoglobin affinity","C":"It contains many chloroplasts in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology","D":"haemoglobin is converted into an enzyme"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-CIRCULATION","scaffold":"Active tissues use oxygen and produce carbon dioxide and heat, shifting the dissociation curve.","reforge":{"stem":"During intense exercise, muscle tissue receives more oxygen from haemoglobin than it does at rest. What conditions in the active muscle cause haemoglobin to release more oxygen there?","options":{"A":"Muscle temperature always falls during exercise in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology","B":"Lower oxygen partial pressure and higher carbon dioxide reduce haemoglobin's affinity for oxygen","C":"The blood stops flowing through capillaries","D":"Haemoglobin is converted into an enzyme"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-N3-01","stem":"The specific immune response differs from the non-specific response because the specific response:","options":{"A":"Acts within minutes of any pathogen entering the body.","B":"Relies entirely on physical barriers such as skin and mucus.","C":"Targets particular antigens and produces memory cells","D":"Involves phagocytosis of any foreign material encountered."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Non-specific defences act the same way against any pathogen and are immediate: physical and chemical barriers (skin, mucus, stomach acid, lysozyme in tears), phagocytosis, and inflammation. Specific defences are slower to start but antigen-specific and leave memory cells: cell-mediated immunity (T lymphocytes) and humoral immunity (B lymphocytes and antibodies). The memory component is what makes vaccination possible.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the secondary immune response faster and larger than the primary response?","options":{"A":"Memory cells are already present and rapidly divide into plasma cells","B":"The pathogen's antigens change to a form more easily recognised.","C":"Antibodies from the primary response remain in the blood permanently.","D":"Phagocytes engulf the pathogen before it can reproduce at all."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.5"},{"id":"BIO-N3-02","stem":"An antibody molecule is best described as a:","options":{"A":"Carbohydrate marker displayed on the surface of a pathogen.","B":"Steroid hormone released by activated T lymphocytes.","C":"Phospholipid that disrupts the bacterial cell membrane.","D":"Protein whose variable regions bind a specific antigen"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Antibodies (immunoglobulins) are Y-shaped proteins of four polypeptide chains — two heavy, two light — held by disulfide bridges. The variable region at the tips differs between antibodies and gives specificity, forming an antigen-antibody complex with a complementary antigen. The constant region is the same within a class and binds phagocyte receptors. Actions include agglutination (clumping pathogens for easier phagocytosis) and neutralising toxins.","reforge":{"stem":"Agglutination by antibodies assists the immune response because it:","options":{"A":"Directly lyses the cell membrane of every bacterium present.","B":"Clumps pathogens so phagocytes engulf many at once","C":"Converts the antigen into a harmless carbohydrate.","D":"Stimulates the pathogen to reproduce more slowly in the blood."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-4.1"},{"id":"BIO-N3-03","stem":"A vaccine produces active immunity because it:","options":{"A":"Stimulates the body to make its own antibodies and memory","B":"Supplies ready-made antibodies taken from an immune donor.","C":"Kills all pathogens already present in the bloodstream.","D":"Provides temporary protection lasting only a few weeks."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Active immunity: the individual's own immune system responds to antigen, so memory cells form and protection is long-lasting. Natural active — infection; artificial active — vaccination. Passive immunity: antibodies come from elsewhere, so protection is immediate but short-lived and no memory cells form. Natural passive — across the placenta or in breast milk; artificial passive — antivenom or immunoglobulin injection.","reforge":{"stem":"A patient bitten by a venomous snake is given antivenom containing antibodies. This provides:","options":{"A":"Natural active immunity, because the body meets the antigen.","B":"Artificial active immunity, because the treatment is injected.","C":"Artificial passive immunity, giving immediate but short-lived protection","D":"Natural passive immunity, because antibodies come from another organism."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-4.1"},{"id":"BIO-N3-04","stem":"Herd immunity protects unvaccinated individuals in a population because:","options":{"A":"Vaccinated individuals produce antibodies that circulate to others.","B":"Enough people are immune that transmission is disrupted","C":"The pathogen mutates into a harmless form within vaccinated hosts.","D":"Unvaccinated individuals develop memory cells without exposure."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Herd immunity works at population level: if a high enough proportion is immune, an infected individual is unlikely to meet a susceptible one, so chains of transmission break. This protects those who cannot be vaccinated — infants, the immunocompromised, those with allergies. The threshold depends on how transmissible the pathogen is (measles needs ~95%, others less). Antibodies are not shared between individuals; only the interruption of transmission is shared.","reforge":{"stem":"Influenza vaccines must be reformulated each year mainly because the virus:","options":{"A":"Loses its outer lipid envelope over time in the population.","B":"Becomes resistant to the antibiotics used alongside vaccination.","C":"Reproduces too slowly to be detected by the immune system.","D":"Undergoes antigenic variation, so memory cells fail to match"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.6"},{"id":"BIO-N3-05","stem":"Antibiotics are ineffective against viral infections because viruses:","options":{"A":"Reproduce far more slowly than bacterial cells do.","B":"Are always enclosed in a protective lipid envelope.","C":"Lack the walls and ribosomes that antibiotics target","D":"Possess a peptidoglycan wall that antibiotics cannot penetrate."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Antibiotics act on features unique to bacteria — peptidoglycan wall synthesis (penicillin), 70S ribosomes (tetracycline, streptomycin), bacterial DNA gyrase. Viruses are not cells: they have no wall, no ribosomes and no metabolism of their own, using host machinery instead, so there is nothing for an antibiotic to target. Antivirals must instead block viral enzymes such as reverse transcriptase or protease. Prescribing antibiotics for viral illness adds no benefit and drives resistance.","reforge":{"stem":"Antibiotic resistance spreads rapidly through bacterial populations partly because bacteria can:","options":{"A":"Transfer resistance genes on plasmids by conjugation","B":"Deliberately mutate in response to the antibiotic present.","C":"Develop immunity using memory cells after first exposure.","D":"Convert the antibiotic into a nutrient source for growth."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-4.1"},{"id":"BIO-N3-06","stem":"Natural selection leads to evolution only when the selected characteristic is:","options":{"A":"Determined solely by the environment rather than by genes.","B":"Acquired during the organism's own lifetime through use.","C":"Present in every individual of the population equally.","D":"Heritable, so advantageous alleles are passed to offspring"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Natural selection requires: variation within the population (ultimately from mutation, plus meiosis and random fertilisation); a selection pressure; differential survival and reproduction; and — crucially — heritability, so the advantageous alleles increase in frequency in the next generation. Acquired characteristics (Lamarck's model) are not inherited. Note that selection acts on phenotypes but changes allele frequencies, and organisms do not mutate 'in order to' adapt.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the correct view of mutation and selection?","options":{"A":"Bacteria mutate in response to an antibiotic in order to survive it.","B":"Mutations occur randomly; the environment then selects among them","C":"Mutation only occurs when a population is under selection pressure.","D":"Selection creates new alleles that were not previously present."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-4.3"},{"id":"BIO-N3-07","stem":"Directional selection differs from stabilising selection in that directional selection:","options":{"A":"Shifts the mean phenotype towards one extreme of the range","B":"Favours individuals with the modal, average phenotype.","C":"Always reduces the total genetic diversity of a population to zero.","D":"Splits one population into two distinct non-breeding species."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Stabilising selection favours intermediate phenotypes and acts against both extremes, reducing variation — human birth mass is the standard example, and it predominates in a stable environment. Directional selection favours one extreme, shifting the mean in that direction — antibiotic resistance and peppered moth colouration are examples, and it predominates when the environment changes. Disruptive selection favours both extremes against the middle and can begin speciation.","reforge":{"stem":"Human birth mass shows a narrow range, with high mortality at both very low and very high masses. This illustrates:","options":{"A":"Disruptive selection favouring both extreme masses.","B":"Directional selection towards a higher birth mass.","C":"Stabilising selection, favouring the mean phenotype","D":"Genetic drift acting on a small isolated population."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-N3-08","stem":"Allopatric speciation begins when two populations become:","options":{"A":"Reproductively isolated by a behavioural difference in the same area.","B":"Geographically separated, so gene flow between them stops","C":"Identical in allele frequency through interbreeding.","D":"Subject to precisely the same selection pressures."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Speciation requires reproductive isolation so gene pools diverge. Allopatric: a geographical barrier (river, mountain, ocean) splits the population; different selection pressures and independent mutation cause divergence until interbreeding is no longer possible. Sympatric: isolation arises without geographical separation — behavioural (different courtship or breeding season), mechanical, or through polyploidy, which is common in plants and can produce a new species in one generation.","reforge":{"stem":"Two plant populations in the same field cannot interbreed because one has become tetraploid. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"Allopatric speciation caused by a geographical barrier.","B":"Genetic drift in a small founder population.","C":"Directional selection acting on flower colour.","D":"Sympatric speciation, without geographical separation"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.6"},{"id":"BIO-N3-09","stem":"Genetic drift has a greater effect on small populations because:","options":{"A":"Small populations experience stronger selection pressures.","B":"Mutation rates per individual are higher in small populations.","C":"Chance changes in allele frequency have larger effects","D":"Small populations always contain more heterozygous individuals."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Genetic drift is random change in allele frequency between generations due to chance in which individuals reproduce. In a large population these fluctuations largely cancel out; in a small one a single chance event can remove an allele entirely. The founder effect (a few individuals colonise a new area) and genetic bottlenecks (population crash) both create small populations with reduced genetic diversity, making them vulnerable to disease and environmental change.","reforge":{"stem":"A population crashes to 20 individuals then recovers to thousands. The likely genetic consequence is:","options":{"A":"Reduced genetic diversity, as alleles were lost in the crash","B":"No change, because population size has been restored.","C":"A permanent change in the mutation rate of every gene.","D":"An increase in the number of alleles present in the population."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.6"},{"id":"BIO-N3-10","stem":"In the Hardy-Weinberg equation, the term 2pq represents the frequency of:","options":{"A":"Homozygous recessive individuals in the population","B":"All individuals showing the dominant phenotype.","C":"The dominant allele within the whole gene pool.","D":"Heterozygous individuals in the population."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"Allele frequencies: p + q = 1, where p is the dominant and q the recessive allele. Genotype frequencies: p² + 2pq + q² = 1, where p² is homozygous dominant, 2pq heterozygous, q² homozygous recessive. Method: the recessive phenotype gives q² directly, so q = √q², then p = 1 − q, then 2pq. Assumptions: large population, random mating, no mutation, no migration, no selection — real populations rarely meet all of these.","reforge":{"stem":"In a population, 16% show the recessive phenotype. What is the frequency of the dominant allele?","options":{"A":"0.16, read directly from the recessive percentage.","B":"0.6, because q = 0.4 and p = 1 − 0.4","C":"0.4, because q² = 0.16 so q = 0.4.","D":"0.84, found by subtracting 0.16 from 1."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.2"},{"id":"BIO-N3-11","stem":"A test cross is carried out to determine whether an organism showing the dominant phenotype is:","options":{"A":"Homozygous dominant or heterozygous for that gene","B":"Carrying a mutation in its mitochondrial DNA.","C":"Male or female in a species without visible dimorphism.","D":"Capable of producing viable gametes at all."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"A test cross crosses the unknown individual with a homozygous recessive. If the unknown is homozygous dominant (AA), all offspring show the dominant phenotype. If heterozygous (Aa), roughly half the offspring show the recessive phenotype — because the recessive parent contributes only recessive alleles, offspring phenotypes reveal the unknown's gametes directly. Remember genotype is the alleles present; phenotype is the characteristic expressed.","reforge":{"stem":"A black guinea pig is crossed with a white (homozygous recessive) one. Half the offspring are white. The black parent's genotype is:","options":{"A":"Homozygous dominant, since black is the dominant phenotype.","B":"Homozygous recessive, since white offspring appeared.","C":"Heterozygous, since it passed on a recessive allele","D":"Impossible to determine from this cross alone."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.2"},{"id":"BIO-N3-12","stem":"A woman who is a carrier for haemophilia (X-linked recessive) has children with an unaffected man. The probability that a son is affected is:","options":{"A":"0%, because males cannot inherit the condition.","B":"50%, because each son inherits one of her two X chromosomes","C":"100%, because sons always inherit the mother's X chromosome.","D":"25%, counting all children of both sexes."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"X-linked recessive: the allele is on the X chromosome. Females (XX) need two copies to be affected and can be carriers; males (XY) have only one X, so a single recessive allele is expressed — which is why such conditions are far commoner in males. Carrier mother XᴴXʰ × unaffected father XᴴY gives XᴴXᴴ, XᴴXʰ, XᴴY, XʰY. So half of sons are affected, half of daughters are carriers, and 25% of all children are affected sons.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are X-linked recessive conditions far more common in males than in females?","options":{"A":"Females cannot inherit X-linked alleles from their fathers.","B":"Males inherit two copies of every X-linked gene.","C":"The Y chromosome carries a second copy of the same allele.","D":"Males have only one X, so one recessive allele is expressed"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.2"},{"id":"BIO-N3-13","stem":"Epistasis occurs when:","options":{"A":"A single gene controls several unrelated characteristics.","B":"Two alleles of the same gene are both fully expressed together.","C":"A gene at one locus masks the expression of another gene","D":"Genes on the same chromosome are inherited together."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETICS","scaffold":"Epistasis — a gene at one locus affects the expression of a gene at a different locus, distorting the expected dihybrid 9:3:3:1 ratio into ratios such as 9:7, 12:3:1 or 9:3:4. Distinguish from: codominance (both alleles of one gene expressed, e.g. AB blood group); multiple alleles (more than two alleles exist for one gene); autosomal linkage (genes on the same chromosome inherited together, reducing recombinants).","reforge":{"stem":"A dihybrid cross gives a 9:7 ratio rather than 9:3:3:1. The most likely explanation is:","options":{"A":"Epistasis, with one gene masking the other","B":"Sex linkage of both genes to the X chromosome.","C":"Codominance between the two alleles of one gene.","D":"Random genetic drift in a small breeding population."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-N3-14","stem":"The three domains of life in modern classification are:","options":{"A":"Plants, animals and fungi.","B":"Kingdom, phylum and class.","C":"Prokaryotes, eukaryotes and viruses.","D":"Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-EVOLUTION","scaffold":"Woese's three-domain system separates Archaea from Bacteria because their rRNA sequences, membrane lipids and cell wall chemistry differ fundamentally — Archaea are in some ways closer to Eukarya. The hierarchy below domain is: kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species. Binomial naming uses genus (capitalised) and species, italicised. Viruses are excluded from this system as they are not cellular.","reforge":{"stem":"Classification based on DNA and RNA sequence comparison is considered more reliable than one based on physical features because:","options":{"A":"Physical features cannot be measured accurately in any organism.","B":"Sequence data reflects evolutionary relatedness directly","C":"DNA sequences are identical within a whole kingdom.","D":"Molecular methods do not require any living specimens."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-4.3"},{"id":"BIO-N3-15","stem":"Species richness alone is an incomplete measure of biodiversity because it ignores:","options":{"A":"The relative abundance of individuals in each species","B":"The geographical size of the habitat being sampled.","C":"The date on which the sampling was carried out.","D":"The total number of different species present in the area."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Species richness counts how many species are present. An index of diversity also weights how evenly individuals are distributed among them: two communities with 5 species each are not equally diverse if one has them in equal numbers and the other is dominated by a single species. Simpson's index D = 1 − Σ(n/N)², where n is the number of individuals of one species and N the total; higher values indicate greater diversity and usually greater stability.","reforge":{"stem":"Two woodlands each contain 6 species. Woodland A has them in roughly equal numbers; woodland B is 90% one species. Which is correct?","options":{"A":"Both have identical diversity because richness is equal.","B":"Woodland B has higher diversity because one species dominates.","C":"Woodland A has higher diversity, being more evenly spread","D":"Diversity cannot be compared without knowing the woodland areas."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-4.2"},{"id":"BIO-N3-16","stem":"Using random quadrats rather than choosing sampling sites by eye is important because it:","options":{"A":"Guarantees every species in the habitat will be recorded.","B":"Removes bias, so the sample represents the habitat","C":"Increases the total number of quadrats that can be placed.","D":"Eliminates the need to repeat the sampling procedure."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"Random sampling (coordinates from random number generation) avoids the unconscious bias of picking interesting-looking patches, so the estimate is representative and can be treated statistically. Use enough quadrats for a reliable mean. Systematic sampling along a transect is used deliberately where an environmental gradient exists (e.g. up a shore), because there the aim is to show how distribution changes rather than to estimate an overall mean.","reforge":{"stem":"A student investigates how plant species change from the sea up a rocky shore. The most appropriate method is:","options":{"A":"Random quadrats scattered across the whole shore area.","B":"A single large quadrat placed at the midpoint of the shore.","C":"Mark-release-recapture using marked individual plants.","D":"A belt transect running from the water's edge inland"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-N3-17","stem":"In the mark-release-recapture method, the population estimate would be too high if:","options":{"A":"Every marked individual were recaptured in the second sample.","B":"The marks were completely waterproof and long-lasting.","C":"Marked individuals were more likely to be eaten or to leave","D":"The two sampling occasions were only one hour apart."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"Estimate = (n₁ × n₂) ÷ m, where n₁ and n₂ are the sample sizes and m the number of marked recaptures. A smaller m inflates the estimate. Assumptions: marking does not affect survival or behaviour, marks are not lost, enough time is allowed for mixing but not so much that births, deaths or migration change the population, and the population is closed. Marks that increase predation reduce m and therefore overestimate the population.","reforge":{"stem":"60 woodlice are marked and released. A later sample of 80 contains 12 marked individuals. The estimated population is:","options":{"A":"400, calculated as (60 × 80) ÷ 12","B":"152, calculated as 60 + 80 + 12.","C":"12, the number of recaptured marked individuals.","D":"48, calculated as 60 × 80 ÷ 100."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.6"},{"id":"BIO-N3-18","stem":"Only about 10% of energy is transferred between trophic levels mainly because:","options":{"A":"Producers convert all incoming light energy into biomass.","B":"Energy is destroyed as it passes along the food chain.","C":"Consumers are unable to digest any plant material at all.","D":"Energy is lost in respiration, excretion and faeces"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Losses between trophic levels: not all of the organism is eaten (roots, bones), not all eaten material is digested (lost in faeces), and much assimilated energy is released as heat during respiration or lost in excretory products such as urea. Energy is transferred and dissipated, never destroyed. These losses explain why food chains rarely exceed 4-5 levels and why a given area supports more people on a plant-based diet than on meat.","reforge":{"stem":"Net primary productivity is best defined as:","options":{"A":"The total light energy falling on the producers in an area.","B":"Gross primary productivity minus respiratory losses","C":"The energy transferred to the primary consumers annually.","D":"The energy lost by producers in respiration each year."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.5"},{"id":"BIO-N3-19","stem":"Nitrogen-fixing bacteria such as Rhizobium benefit plants by converting:","options":{"A":"Atmospheric nitrogen into usable ammonium compounds","B":"Nitrates in the soil into nitrogen gas released to the air.","C":"Ammonium compounds into nitrites and then nitrates.","D":"Plant proteins into ammonia during decomposition."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Nitrogen cycle roles: nitrogen fixation (Rhizobium in root nodules, free-living Azotobacter) — N₂ to ammonium; ammonification (saprobionts) — organic nitrogen in dead matter and urea to ammonium; nitrification (Nitrosomonas then Nitrobacter, both requiring oxygen) — ammonium to nitrite to nitrate; denitrification (anaerobic bacteria in waterlogged soil) — nitrate back to N₂, reducing soil fertility. Ploughing aerates soil, favouring nitrification over denitrification.","reforge":{"stem":"Waterlogged soil becomes less fertile largely because anaerobic conditions favour:","options":{"A":"Nitrogen fixation by free-living soil bacteria.","B":"Nitrifying bacteria, which require oxygen to function.","C":"Denitrifying bacteria, converting nitrate to nitrogen","D":"Saprobiotic decomposition of dead organic material."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-N3-20","stem":"Eutrophication of a river following fertiliser runoff leads to fish deaths because:","options":{"A":"Nitrates are directly toxic to fish at any concentration.","B":"Algal blooms block light, and decomposers deplete oxygen","C":"Algal blooms increase the oxygen concentration to lethal levels.","D":"Fertiliser lowers the water temperature below the fish's tolerance."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Sequence: leached nitrate and phosphate enrich the water → algal bloom at the surface → light is blocked from submerged plants, which die → saprobiotic bacteria decompose the dead plants and algae, multiplying rapidly → aerobic respiration by these bacteria depletes dissolved oxygen (measured as high BOD) → fish and invertebrates suffocate. The oxygen depletion, not the nitrate itself, is what kills the fish.","reforge":{"stem":"A high biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) in a water sample indicates:","options":{"A":"A high concentration of dissolved oxygen available to fish.","B":"A low rate of decomposition of organic material.","C":"That the water contains no organic pollution at all.","D":"A large population of aerobic microbes using oxygen"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.5"},{"id":"BIO-N3-21","stem":"Primary succession differs from secondary succession because primary succession:","options":{"A":"Always ends in a grassland rather than a woodland climax.","B":"Occurs only after a fire has destroyed existing vegetation.","C":"Begins on bare rock or newly exposed ground with no soil","D":"Involves no change in species diversity over time."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Primary succession starts on newly formed or exposed surfaces with no soil (lava flows, sand dunes, bare rock). Pioneer species (lichens, mosses) tolerate extreme conditions, weather the rock and add organic matter on death, forming soil. Each seral stage changes abiotic conditions, allowing larger species that outcompete the previous ones, until a stable climax community forms. Secondary succession begins where soil already exists, so it proceeds faster.","reforge":{"stem":"Pioneer species are essential to primary succession because they:","options":{"A":"Weather the rock and add organic matter, forming soil","B":"Prevent any other species from colonising the area later.","C":"Are the tallest and most competitive species in the sere.","D":"Reduce the biodiversity of the developing community."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.5"},{"id":"BIO-N3-22","stem":"Conservation of a habitat differs from preservation because conservation:","options":{"A":"Leaves the habitat entirely undisturbed by any intervention.","B":"Prevents all human access to the protected area.","C":"Applies only to species that are already extinct in the wild.","D":"Involves active management of a habitat still in use"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"Preservation means leaving an area untouched, excluding human activity. Conservation is dynamic management that maintains biodiversity while allowing sustainable human use — coppicing, controlled grazing, rotational felling, replanting, seed banks, captive breeding and reintroduction. Conservation reasons are ecological (ecosystem stability, genetic resources), economic (medicines, crops, tourism) and ethical. Succession is often deliberately arrested to preserve a valued seral stage such as heathland.","reforge":{"stem":"Grazing is used to maintain chalk grassland. Removing the grazing animals would cause the habitat to:","options":{"A":"Remain permanently unchanged as grassland.","B":"Undergo succession towards scrub and woodland","C":"Revert immediately to bare rock with no soil.","D":"Increase its grassland species diversity indefinitely."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.6"},{"id":"BIO-N3-23","stem":"HIV eventually causes AIDS because the virus destroys:","options":{"A":"T helper cells, so the specific response is not coordinated","B":"Red blood cells, preventing oxygen transport to tissues.","C":"Platelets, so the blood is unable to clot normally.","D":"Phagocytes, removing all non-specific defence against pathogens."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-ECOLOGY-DISEASE","scaffold":"HIV is a retrovirus: it binds CD4 receptors on T helper cells, reverse transcriptase copies its RNA into DNA, which integrates into the host genome. Replication destroys T helper cells. Because T helper cells activate B cells, cytotoxic T cells and phagocytes, losing them disables the whole specific response. AIDS is the resulting condition in which opportunistic infections and cancers take hold. Antiretrovirals target reverse transcriptase and protease.","reforge":{"stem":"Reverse transcriptase is a useful drug target in HIV treatment because it:","options":{"A":"Is required by all human cells for normal DNA replication.","B":"Forms the outer protein coat surrounding the viral RNA.","C":"Catalyses a step unique to the virus, absent in humans","D":"Binds the virus to the CD4 receptor on the host cell."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-N3-24","stem":"A monoclonal antibody is described as a useful diagnostic tool because it:","options":{"A":"Destroys every pathogen present in a blood sample.","B":"Binds one specific antigen, allowing precise detection","C":"Is produced naturally by all lymphocytes in the body.","D":"Reacts with a wide range of unrelated antigens at once."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETIC-ENGINEERING","scaffold":"Monoclonal antibodies are identical copies from a single B cell clone, made by fusing an antibody-producing B cell with a myeloma (tumour) cell to form a hybridoma that both secretes antibody and divides indefinitely. Their single specificity supports pregnancy testing (detecting hCG), ELISA diagnostics, and targeted cancer therapy where a drug is attached to an antibody specific to a tumour cell antigen, reducing damage to healthy tissue.","reforge":{"stem":"In an ELISA test, an enzyme is attached to the second antibody in order to:","options":{"A":"Destroy any antigen that has not bound to the first antibody.","B":"Prevent the antibody from binding to the wrong antigen.","C":"Increase the specificity of the first antibody's binding site.","D":"Produce a colour change showing that the antigen is present"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.4"},{"id":"BIO-N3-25","stem":"A restriction endonuclease is used in genetic engineering to:","options":{"A":"Produce DNA from an mRNA template in a single step.","B":"Join the sugar-phosphate backbones of two DNA fragments.","C":"Cut DNA at a specific sequence, often leaving sticky ends","D":"Amplify a DNA fragment through repeated heating and cooling."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETIC-ENGINEERING","scaffold":"Toolkit: restriction endonuclease cuts at a palindromic recognition sequence, often producing staggered sticky ends; DNA ligase joins sugar-phosphate backbones; reverse transcriptase makes cDNA from mRNA (useful because it lacks introns); PCR amplifies fragments using primers, DNA polymerase and thermal cycling. Cutting the gene and the plasmid vector with the SAME enzyme is what makes their sticky ends complementary so they anneal.","reforge":{"stem":"Why must the same restriction enzyme be used to cut both the gene and the plasmid vector?","options":{"A":"So the sticky ends are complementary and can base-pair together","B":"So that DNA ligase is not required to complete the joining.","C":"So that the plasmid loses its antibiotic resistance marker gene.","D":"So that both fragments are cut into pieces of identical length."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.3"},{"id":"BIO-N3-26","stem":"In the polymerase chain reaction, the mixture is heated to about 95°C in order to:","options":{"A":"Activate the primers so they can bind to the template.","B":"Allow Taq polymerase to extend the new DNA strands.","C":"Destroy any contaminating enzymes in the reaction tube.","D":"Break hydrogen bonds, separating the two DNA strands"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETIC-ENGINEERING","scaffold":"PCR cycle: denaturation ~95°C separates strands by breaking hydrogen bonds; annealing ~55°C lets primers bind either side of the target sequence; extension ~72°C lets Taq polymerase build new strands from free nucleotides. Taq comes from a thermophilic bacterium so it survives the denaturation step — ordinary DNA polymerase would denature. Each cycle doubles the DNA, so n cycles give 2ⁿ copies.","reforge":{"stem":"Starting with one DNA molecule, how many copies exist after 5 complete PCR cycles?","options":{"A":"10, because each cycle adds two more copies.","B":"32, because the quantity doubles each cycle","C":"5, one for each cycle completed.","D":"25, because the number is squared each cycle."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-N3-27","stem":"Gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments principally according to their:","options":{"A":"Length, because shorter fragments travel further","B":"Ability to bind to a specific gene probe.","C":"Base sequence, with A-T rich fragments moving fastest.","D":"Total number of hydrogen bonds between the two strands."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETIC-ENGINEERING","scaffold":"DNA is negatively charged because of its phosphate groups, so it moves towards the anode when a voltage is applied across the gel. The gel acts as a molecular sieve: short fragments travel further in a given time, long ones lag. Fragments of known size run alongside as a ladder for comparison. Bands are then visualised with a stain or by using labelled gene probes that hybridise to complementary sequences.","reforge":{"stem":"DNA moves towards the positive electrode during electrophoresis because DNA:","options":{"A":"Is heated to break the hydrogen bonds before loading.","B":"Binds to positively charged dye molecules in the gel.","C":"Carries a negative charge due to its phosphate groups","D":"Contains more purine than pyrimidine bases overall."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"BIO-N3-28","stem":"Genetic fingerprinting is possible because individuals differ in the:","options":{"A":"Sequence of the genes coding for their enzymes.","B":"Number of repeats in non-coding regions of their DNA","C":"Total number of chromosomes present in each body cell.","D":"Type of nitrogenous bases found within their DNA."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETIC-ENGINEERING","scaffold":"Most of the human genome is non-coding, and includes short tandem repeats (VNTRs/STRs) whose repeat number varies greatly between individuals while being inherited from both parents. Method: extract DNA, amplify the repeat regions by PCR, separate by electrophoresis, and compare band patterns. Applications include forensic identification, paternity testing and establishing relatedness in conservation breeding programmes. Identical twins share the same pattern.","reforge":{"stem":"In a paternity test, the child's genetic fingerprint should show bands that:","options":{"A":"Contain twice as many bands as either parent's profile.","B":"Are entirely absent from both parents' profiles.","C":"Match the mother's profile exactly, band for band.","D":"Are all matched in the mother's or the father's profile"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-3","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.1"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-BIO3-01","stem":"During reproductive cloning, the nucleus inserted into an enucleated egg provides:","options":{"A":"a new set of random alleles","B":"the egg's mitochondria only","C":"the donor's nuclear genetic material","D":"the embryo's fully developed tissues"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETIC-ENGINEERING","scaffold":"The inserted nucleus carries the donor cell's nuclear genome.","reforge":{"stem":"In reproductive cloning, the nucleus from a donor's body cell is transferred into an egg cell that has had its own nucleus removed. What does this donor nucleus provide to the resulting embryo?","options":{"A":"Fully differentiated tissue taken directly from the donor","B":"Only the mitochondria originally present in the egg","C":"A freshly shuffled set of random alleles","D":"A complete copy of the donor's nuclear DNA"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.4"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-BIO3-02","stem":"One potential benefit of therapeutic cloning is the production of:","options":{"A":"identical whole ecosystems","B":"A technique producing proteins without transcription","C":"gametes with no chromosomes","D":"cells genetically matched to a patient"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-GENETIC-ENGINEERING","scaffold":"Matched cells may reduce immune rejection when used to replace damaged tissue.","reforge":{"stem":"Therapeutic cloning is used to produce embryonic stem cells that share the same genetic material as a specific patient. What is one potential medical benefit of this?","options":{"A":"Cells genetically matched to a patient, reducing rejection risk","B":"Identical whole ecosystems","C":"Gametes with no chromosomes","D":"Antibiotics produced from mineral salts in a typical cell in this biological system"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-6.4"}]},"BIO-ENZ":{"label":"2.4 Enzymes","color":"#15803d","questions":[{"id":"BIO-ENZ-01","spec":"BIO-2.4.1","stem":"A student claims: 'The active site of an enzyme is a rigid, fixed shape that is complementary to its substrate.' Which statement best evaluates this claim?","options":{"A":"The claim is partially correct — the active site is complementary but changes shape slightly when the substrate binds.","B":"The claim is correct — the active site is pre-formed and unchanged by substrate binding.","C":"Muscle temperature always falls during exercise in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology","D":"The claim is correct — complementarity means no conformational change is needed."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"The induced fit model proposes that the active site is NOT a fixed rigid structure. When the substrate enters, the enzyme undergoes a conformational change — the active site moulds around the substrate to form a closer fit, creating the enzyme-substrate complex. This differs from the lock-and-key model, which assumes a rigid, pre-formed complementary shape. The active site IS complementary to the substrate, but it is not rigid.","reforge":{"stem":"An enzyme's active site changes shape when the substrate binds. This best describes which model of enzyme action?","options":{"A":"Lock-and-key model — the active site is a rigid complementary shape.","B":"Competitive inhibition — substrate and inhibitor compete for a rigid active site.","C":"Induced fit model — the active site moulds around the substrate upon binding.","D":"Allosteric model — an inhibitor changes the shape of the active site."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-ENZ-02","spec":"BIO-2.4.2","stem":"At temperatures above 40°C, the rate of an enzyme-controlled reaction decreases sharply. Which explanation is most accurate?","options":{"A":"The enzyme's active site becomes too cold to bind the substrate.","B":"Thermal energy breaks bonds holding the tertiary structure and active site","C":"High temperatures reduce substrate kinetic energy, lowering collision frequency.","D":"The substrate denatures and can no longer fit the active site."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Above the optimum temperature, increased thermal energy breaks the hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds and disulfide bridges that maintain the enzyme's tertiary structure. This changes the shape of the active site so the substrate can no longer bind — the enzyme is denatured. Key point: it is the ENZYME that denatures, not the substrate. The substrate's kinetic energy actually increases with temperature up to this point, which is why the rate initially rises.","reforge":{"stem":"Which bonds are disrupted when an enzyme is denatured by high temperature?","options":{"A":"The claim is correct — the active site is pre-formed and unchanged by substrate binding.","B":"Glycosidic bonds between glucose monomers in the active site.","C":"Phosphodiester bonds between nucleotides in the enzyme's DNA.","D":"Hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds and disulfide bridges that maintain the tertiary structure."},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-ENZ-03","spec":"BIO-2.4.3","stem":"An enzyme has an optimum pH of 7. A student increases the pH to 10. Which of the following correctly explains the effect on enzyme activity?","options":{"A":"Enzyme activity increases because alkaline conditions provide more OH⁻ ions to speed up the reaction.","B":"Enzyme activity decreases because the substrate denatures and can no longer enter the active site.","C":"Activity falls as ionic and hydrogen bonds break, altering the active site","D":"Enzyme activity is unchanged because pH only affects prokaryotic enzymes."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"pH affects enzyme activity because H⁺ and OH⁻ ions interfere with the ionic bonds and hydrogen bonds that maintain the enzyme's tertiary structure. At pH values far from the optimum, these bonds are disrupted and the shape of the active site changes — so the substrate can no longer bind. Crucially, it is the ENZYME structure that changes, not the substrate. The substrate does not denature.","reforge":{"stem":"Which bonds are most affected when the pH is changed far from an enzyme's optimum?","options":{"A":"Hydrogen bonds and ionic bonds in the tertiary structure.","B":"Peptide bonds in the primary structure.","C":"Ester bonds in the phospholipid bilayer surrounding the enzyme.","D":"Glycosidic bonds in the substrate molecule."},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-ENZ-04","spec":"BIO-2.4.4","stem":"As substrate concentration increases, the rate of an enzyme-controlled reaction rises and then levels off into a plateau. What does the plateau represent?","options":{"A":"The rate increases then plateaus immediately — doubling enzyme has no effect when substrate is in excess.","B":"High substrate concentration is now inhibiting the enzyme.","C":"The enzyme has denatured due to excess substrate.","D":"Every enzyme active site is occupied — the enzyme is saturated and working at its maximum rate (Vmax)."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"The plateau represents Vmax — the maximum rate of reaction. At this point every active site is occupied at any given moment (the enzyme is saturated). Adding more substrate cannot increase the rate further because there are no free active sites available. The substrate is NOT used up — there is excess substrate present. To increase the rate beyond Vmax, you would need to add more enzyme.","reforge":{"stem":"A student adds more substrate to a reaction that has already reached Vmax. What happens to the rate of reaction?","options":{"A":"Enzyme activity increases because alkaline conditions provide more OH⁻ ions to speed up the reaction.","B":"It stays the same because all active sites are already occupied — the enzyme is saturated.","C":"It decreases because excess substrate blocks the active sites.","D":"It increases then decreases due to product inhibition."},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-ENZ-05","spec":"BIO-2.4.4","stem":"Enzyme X has a Km of 0.5 mmol dm⁻³ and Enzyme Y has a Km of 5.0 mmol dm⁻³. Both act on the same substrate. Which statement is correct?","options":{"A":"Enzyme X — a lower Km means it binds substrate more easily at lower concentration.","B":"Enzyme Y has higher affinity because it has a higher Km.","C":"Induced fit means one enzyme can catalyse any substrate because the site is adaptable.","D":"Km only reflects maximum reaction rate, not affinity."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Km is the substrate concentration at which the reaction rate equals half of Vmax. A LOW Km means the enzyme reaches half its maximum rate at a low substrate concentration — it binds substrate easily, indicating HIGH affinity. A HIGH Km means more substrate is needed to half-saturate the enzyme — indicating LOW affinity. Km and affinity are inversely related: low Km = high affinity. Enzyme X (Km 0.5) has ten times the affinity of Enzyme Y (Km 5.0).","reforge":{"stem":"Under conditions of very low substrate concentration, which enzyme would be most active — one with Km of 0.2 mmol dm⁻³ or one with Km of 2.0 mmol dm⁻³?","options":{"A":"It removes the need for identification in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology","B":"Both would be equally active at low substrate concentrations.","C":"The enzyme with Km of 0.2, because its high affinity allows it to bind substrate even at low concentrations.","D":"Neither — both enzymes require high substrate concentrations to function."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-ENZ-06","spec":"BIO-2.4.6","stem":"A competitive inhibitor is added to an enzyme-catalysed reaction. Which row correctly describes the effect on Vmax and Km?","options":{"A":"Vmax: decreased · Km: unchanged","B":"Vmax: unchanged · Km: increased","C":"Vmax: decreased · Km: increased","D":"Vmax: unchanged · Km: unchanged"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"A competitive inhibitor has a similar shape to the substrate and competes for the active site. At high enough substrate concentrations, the substrate outcompetes the inhibitor — so Vmax is UNCHANGED (the enzyme can still reach maximum rate given enough substrate). However, at any given substrate concentration, the inhibitor reduces the chance of substrate binding, so MORE substrate is needed to achieve half-Vmax. This means Km INCREASES. Memory aid: competitive inhibition = Vmax unchanged, Km increased.","reforge":{"stem":"A non-competitive inhibitor is added to the same reaction. How does the effect differ from competitive inhibition?","options":{"A":"Vmax decreases, Km decreases — the enzyme binds substrate more tightly.","B":"Vmax unchanged, Km increases — the same as competitive inhibition.","C":"Both Vmax and Km increase.","D":"Vmax falls, Km unchanged: the inhibitor cannot be outcompeted"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-ENZ-07","spec":"BIO-2.4.7","stem":"A non-competitive inhibitor binds to the allosteric site of an enzyme. Which statement correctly describes the mechanism?","options":{"A":"The inhibitor blocks the active site directly, preventing substrate from binding.","B":"Enzyme activity decreases because the substrate denatures and can no longer enter the active site.","C":"It changes the active site's shape via the enzyme's tertiary structure, reducing activity.","D":"The inhibitor only acts at temperatures above the enzyme's optimum."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"A non-competitive inhibitor binds at the allosteric site (not the active site). This causes a conformational change in the enzyme's tertiary structure, which alters the shape of the active site. The substrate can still enter the active site, but the enzyme-substrate complex forms less effectively. Crucially, adding more substrate CANNOT overcome non-competitive inhibition — unlike competitive inhibition. Vmax decreases; Km is unchanged.","reforge":{"stem":"A student says: 'Adding more substrate will reverse the effect of a non-competitive inhibitor.' Is this correct?","options":{"A":"No — the non-competitive inhibitor binds at the allosteric site, not the active site. More substrate cannot displace it.","B":"Yes — more substrate outcompetes the inhibitor for the active site.","C":"Yes — high substrate concentration causes the allosteric site to close.","D":"It is irrelevant — phenotype and genotype always correspond directly. The biological explanation must account for the cells, molecules, structures, mechanisms and conditions stated; the answer treats an exception or consequence as though it were the underlying principle."},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-ENZ-08","spec":"BIO-2.4.8","stem":"An enzyme requires a zinc ion (Zn²⁺) permanently bound to its structure in order to function. The Zn²⁺ ion is best described as:","options":{"A":"Enzyme activity increases because alkaline conditions provide more OH⁻ ions to speed up the reaction.","B":"A competitive inhibitor — the ion occupies the active site.","C":"A substrate — the ion is changed during the reaction.","D":"A prosthetic group — a non-protein component permanently and tightly bound to the enzyme."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"A prosthetic group is a non-protein component that is permanently and tightly bound to the enzyme — essential for its function. A coenzyme is also non-protein but binds temporarily and is often shared between different enzymes (e.g. NAD⁺, FAD). A cofactor is the broad term covering both. Since Zn²⁺ is permanently bound, it is a prosthetic group. This distinction is tested by OCR examiners — FAD is sometimes considered a prosthetic group; NAD is a coenzyme.","reforge":{"stem":"NAD⁺ is an organic molecule that temporarily associates with enzymes during respiration and carries hydrogen. NAD⁺ is best described as:","options":{"A":"A prosthetic group — permanently bound to the enzyme.","B":"A coenzyme — a non-protein organic molecule that temporarily binds to the enzyme and carries groups between reactions.","C":"A substrate — NAD⁺ is permanently changed during the reaction.","D":"It is irrelevant — phenotype and genotype always correspond directly. The biological explanation must account for the cells, molecules, structures, mechanisms and conditions stated; the answer treats an exception or consequence as though it were the underlying principle."},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-ENZ-09","spec":"BIO-2.4.5","stem":"An experiment investigates the effect of enzyme concentration on rate of reaction, keeping substrate concentration constant and in excess. As enzyme concentration doubles, what happens to the initial rate of reaction?","options":{"A":"The rate approximately doubles — more active sites are available to bind substrate molecules simultaneously.","B":"The rate decreases — more enzyme molecules compete for the same substrate molecules.","C":"The rate stays the same — all active sites are already occupied by the excess substrate.","D":"It removes the need for identification in a typical cell in this biological system according to standard biology"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"When substrate is in excess, the rate of reaction is proportional to enzyme concentration. Doubling the enzyme doubles the number of active sites available, so more enzyme-substrate complexes can form simultaneously — approximately doubling the rate. The substrate is in excess so there is no shortage of substrate molecules to occupy the new active sites. Compare this with the effect of increasing substrate concentration, where the rate plateaus at Vmax when enzyme becomes the limiting factor.","reforge":{"stem":"In the same experiment, substrate concentration is then reduced to a very low level. What now limits the rate of reaction?","options":{"A":"Enzyme concentration — there are not enough active sites.","B":"Temperature — low substrate concentration reduces thermal energy.","C":"Substrate concentration — few substrate molecules are available to enter the active sites.","D":"Enzyme activity decreases because the substrate denatures and can no longer enter the active site."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-ENZ-10","spec":"BIO-2.4.2","stem":"The Q₁₀ of an enzyme-controlled reaction is calculated as 2. What does this mean?","options":{"A":"The reaction requires a temperature of exactly 20°C to function.","B":"For every 10°C rise in temperature, the rate of reaction doubles.","C":"The enzyme has an optimum temperature of 20°C above room temperature.","D":"For every 2°C rise in temperature, the rate doubles."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Q₁₀ is the temperature coefficient — the factor by which the rate of reaction changes when the temperature is raised by 10°C. Q₁₀ = rate at (T+10°C) / rate at T°C. For most enzyme-controlled reactions Q₁₀ ≈ 2, meaning the rate approximately doubles for every 10°C rise (up to the optimum). This is because higher temperatures increase the kinetic energy of both enzyme and substrate molecules, increasing the frequency of successful collisions. Above the optimum, denaturation dominates and rate falls.","reforge":{"stem":"At 20°C, an enzyme-controlled reaction has a rate of 50 μmol/min. Given Q₁₀ = 2, what would the expected rate be at 40°C?","options":{"A":"100 μmol/min — one 10°C step doubles the rate.","B":"Hydrolysed — water molecules break peptide bonds within the enzyme structure.","C":"400 μmol/min — Q₁₀ applies to the full 40°C range.","D":"200 μmol/min — two 10°C steps, each doubling the rate: 50 × 2 × 2 = 200."},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-E4-01","spec":"BIO-ENZ","stem":"A competitive inhibitor reduces enzyme activity by:","options":{"A":"increasing substrate concentration automatically","B":"destroying every enzyme molecule","C":"competing with substrate for the active site","D":"binding only to DNA"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Increasing substrate concentration can reduce the effect of a competitive inhibitor.","reforge":{"stem":"A molecule with a shape similar to an enzyme's normal substrate binds to the active site, blocking the substrate from entering. How does this type of inhibitor reduce enzyme activity?","options":{"A":"By increasing substrate concentration automatically","B":"By competing with the substrate for the active site","C":"By destroying every enzyme molecule","D":"By binding only to DNA"},"correct":"B"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-E4-02","spec":"BIO-ENZ","stem":"At very high temperature, enzyme activity falls mainly because:","options":{"A":"pH affects the solubility of the substrate rather than the enzyme.","B":"water potential increases","C":"the enzyme gains more active sites","D":"bonds maintaining the active-site shape are disrupted"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Denaturation changes the tertiary structure and active-site shape.","reforge":{"stem":"An enzyme's reaction rate rises with temperature up to a point, then falls sharply as the temperature climbs further. What is happening to the enzyme at these higher temperatures?","options":{"A":"Bonds maintaining the active-site shape are disrupted","B":"Substrate concentration becomes zero in a typical cell","C":"The enzyme gains more active sites","D":"Water potential increases"},"correct":"A"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-E4-03","spec":"BIO-ENZ","stem":"A controlled variable in an enzyme investigation is:","options":{"A":"a factor kept constant so its effect is not confused with the independent variable","B":"Because using fewer decimal places reduces the uncertainty in a typical cell in this biological system","C":"the measured rate","D":"the final conclusion"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"Temperature, pH or enzyme concentration may need to be controlled depending on the design.","reforge":{"stem":"A student investigates how pH affects enzyme activity, changing only the pH between trials. Which of the following best describes a variable they must keep constant?","options":{"A":"The measured rate of reaction","B":"The factor deliberately changed between trials","C":"A controlled variable, kept constant so its effect is not confused with pH","D":"The final conclusion drawn from the investigation in a typical cell in this biological system"},"correct":"C"},"specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-01","stem":"Enzymes increase the rate of a reaction because they:","options":{"A":"Raise the temperature of the reacting solution locally.","B":"Lower the activation energy needed for the reaction","C":"Are consumed as the reaction proceeds to completion.","D":"Increase the total energy released by the reaction overall."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"An enzyme provides an alternative route with a lower activation energy, so a greater proportion of collisions have enough energy at any given temperature. It does not change the overall energy difference between substrate and product, does not make an energetically unfavourable reaction favourable, and is unchanged at the end — so a small quantity can catalyse many reactions. Enzymes are biological catalysts, and each is specific to a substrate.","reforge":{"stem":"On an energy level diagram, the effect of an enzyme is shown by:","options":{"A":"The products being at a lower energy level than before.","B":"A larger difference in energy between reactants and products.","C":"A higher peak between reactants and products.","D":"A lower peak between reactants and products"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-02","stem":"The induced fit model improves on the lock and key model because it proposes that the:","options":{"A":"Substrate changes shape to fit a rigid, unchanging active site.","B":"Enzyme binds any substrate regardless of its shape.","C":"Active site changes shape slightly as the substrate binds","D":"Active site is formed only from the primary structure of the protein."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Lock and key: the active site is a fixed complementary shape. Induced fit: the active site is flexible and moulds around the substrate as it binds, and this conformational change strains the substrate's bonds, which helps lower activation energy. Induced fit explains why some enzymes act on a small range of similar substrates, and why the fit improves after initial binding. Both models require the active site's shape to be determined by tertiary structure.","reforge":{"stem":"Enzyme specificity ultimately depends on which level of protein structure?","options":{"A":"Tertiary structure, which sets the active site's shape","B":"Secondary structure, since α-helices form the active site.","C":"Primary structure alone, since it lists the amino acids.","D":"Quaternary structure, which all enzymes must possess."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-03","stem":"Above its optimum temperature, enzyme activity falls sharply because:","options":{"A":"The activation energy of the reaction increases with temperature.","B":"The substrate molecules move too quickly to collide with the enzyme.","C":"The enzyme's primary structure is hydrolysed into amino acids.","D":"Vibration breaks bonds holding the tertiary structure together"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Below the optimum, raising temperature increases kinetic energy, so successful collisions and enzyme-substrate complex formation increase. Above the optimum, increased vibration breaks the hydrogen and ionic bonds maintaining tertiary structure; the active site is no longer complementary and the enzyme denatures. Denaturation is usually irreversible; peptide bonds are unaffected, so the primary structure remains intact — a common misconception in exam answers.","reforge":{"stem":"Denaturation by heat is usually irreversible, whereas a small pH change may be reversible. This is because heat:","options":{"A":"Removes the substrate from the reaction mixture entirely.","B":"Disrupts many bonds, so the shape cannot re-form","C":"Only affects enzymes that have a quaternary structure.","D":"Converts the enzyme into a different type of protein."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-04","stem":"A pH far from an enzyme's optimum reduces activity because changes in H⁺ concentration:","options":{"A":"Disrupt ionic bonds between charged R-groups","B":"Increase the activation energy required for the reaction.","C":"Reduce the number of substrate molecules present in solution.","D":"Convert the enzyme's active site into a second binding site."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"H⁺ and OH⁻ ions interact with the charged R-groups (-NH₃⁺, -COO⁻) that form ionic bonds and hydrogen bonds in the tertiary structure. Altering the charges disrupts these bonds, so the active site changes shape and the substrate no longer fits. Small deviations are often reversible; large ones denature the enzyme. Optima vary with environment: pepsin ~pH 2 in the stomach, trypsin ~pH 8 in the duodenum, most intracellular enzymes near pH 7.","reforge":{"stem":"Pepsin has an optimum of about pH 2 while trypsin's is about pH 8. This is because each enzyme:","options":{"A":"Contains a different number of active sites per molecule.","B":"Acts on a substrate that only exists at that pH.","C":"Functions in a region of the gut with that pH","D":"Has no tertiary structure at any other pH value."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-05","stem":"In an enzyme-catalysed reaction with excess substrate, increasing enzyme concentration increases the rate because:","options":{"A":"Each enzyme molecule is used up faster at higher concentration.","B":"More active sites are available for enzyme-substrate complexes","C":"The activation energy falls further as enzyme concentration rises.","D":"The substrate becomes the limiting factor immediately."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"With substrate in excess, rate is proportional to enzyme concentration — more active sites means more complexes per second, and the graph is a straight line through the origin. If substrate becomes limiting the line plateaus. Conversely, at fixed enzyme concentration, increasing substrate raises the rate until all active sites are continuously occupied (saturation), after which the rate plateaus at Vmax and only more enzyme will increase it.","reforge":{"stem":"A graph of rate against substrate concentration plateaus at high substrate concentration because:","options":{"A":"The substrate begins to inhibit the enzyme competitively.","B":"The enzyme has denatured at that concentration.","C":"The activation energy has risen to its maximum value.","D":"All active sites are occupied, so the enzyme is saturated"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-06","stem":"A competitive inhibitor reduces enzyme activity because it:","options":{"A":"Permanently denatures the tertiary structure of the enzyme.","B":"Binds to the substrate rather than to the enzyme itself.","C":"Has a shape like the substrate and occupies the active site","D":"Increases the enzyme's affinity for its normal substrate."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Competitive inhibitor: structurally similar to the substrate, binds reversibly at the active site, blocking substrate entry. Its effect is reduced by raising substrate concentration, since substrate then out-competes it — so Vmax is eventually still reached, just more slowly. Non-competitive inhibitor: binds at an allosteric site elsewhere, changing the active site's shape; raising substrate concentration does not overcome it, so Vmax is genuinely lowered.","reforge":{"stem":"Adding more substrate restores the rate in one inhibited reaction but not in another. The second reaction most likely involves:","options":{"A":"A non-competitive inhibitor bound at an allosteric site","B":"A competitive inhibitor with a shape like the substrate.","C":"An enzyme operating below its optimum temperature.","D":"A reaction that has reached chemical equilibrium."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-07","stem":"End-product inhibition is biologically useful because it:","options":{"A":"Increases the rate of the pathway when product levels are high.","B":"Permanently destroys the enzymes once the product is formed.","C":"Allows the pathway to run in reverse when substrate is scarce.","D":"Regulates a pathway according to how much product is needed"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"In end-product (feedback) inhibition, the final product of a pathway acts as a non-competitive inhibitor of an enzyme early in that pathway, usually the first committed step. When product accumulates, the pathway slows; when product is used up, inhibition lifts and the pathway resumes. This prevents wasteful overproduction and accumulation of intermediates, and is a negative feedback mechanism — the same control logic as in homeostasis.","reforge":{"stem":"End-product inhibition is described as negative feedback because the:","options":{"A":"Product increases the activity of the enzyme that made it.","B":"Rising product level reduces the process producing it","C":"Enzyme is destroyed each time product concentration rises.","D":"Pathway operates only when the product is entirely absent."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-08","stem":"Many enzymes require a cofactor. A coenzyme differs from a prosthetic group in that a coenzyme:","options":{"A":"Is an organic molecule that binds only temporarily","B":"Forms the active site of the enzyme it assists.","C":"Permanently inhibits the enzyme after a single reaction.","D":"Is always an inorganic ion such as chloride or zinc."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Cofactor is the umbrella term. Inorganic cofactors are ions — chloride ions for amylase, zinc for carbonic anhydrase. Organic cofactors are coenzymes, often vitamin-derived (NAD, FAD, coenzyme A), which bind temporarily, carry chemical groups or electrons between enzymes, and are then recycled. A prosthetic group is a cofactor permanently bound to the enzyme, such as the zinc ion in carbonic anhydrase or haem in catalase.","reforge":{"stem":"NAD acts as a coenzyme in respiration by:","options":{"A":"Providing a permanent active site for dehydrogenase enzymes.","B":"Catalysing the hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and phosphate.","C":"Accepting hydrogen atoms and carrying them to the chain","D":"Acting as a non-competitive inhibitor of the Krebs cycle."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-09","stem":"Immobilised enzymes are widely used in industry mainly because they:","options":{"A":"Work considerably faster than free enzymes in solution.","B":"Can be recovered and reused, leaving product pure","C":"Are unaffected by any change in temperature or pH.","D":"Do not require a substrate to catalyse their reaction."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-ENZYMES","scaffold":"Immobilisation traps enzymes in alginate beads, on membranes or on inert supports. Advantages: the enzyme is easily separated and reused, product is not contaminated so purification costs fall, the process can run continuously, and stability to temperature and pH is improved. Disadvantages: setup cost, and the rate is often slightly lower because substrate must diffuse to the trapped enzyme. Lactose-free milk production is the standard example.","reforge":{"stem":"Immobilised enzymes often show a slightly lower reaction rate than free enzymes because:","options":{"A":"The enzyme denatures immediately on immobilisation.","B":"The active site is destroyed by the alginate gel.","C":"Immobilised enzymes require a higher activation energy.","D":"Substrate must diffuse into the bead to reach the site"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-10","stem":"In an investigation of catalase activity, oxygen volume is collected every 30 seconds. The initial rate of reaction is best found from:","options":{"A":"The total volume of gas collected by the end of the experiment.","B":"The mean volume collected across all time intervals.","C":"The gradient of the tangent at the start of the curve","D":"The time taken for the reaction to stop completely."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"Rate falls over time as substrate is used up, so a single overall figure understates the true enzyme activity. The initial rate — the gradient at t = 0, where substrate is still in excess — is the fair basis for comparing conditions. Draw a tangent at the origin and calculate its gradient (volume ÷ time). Where a curve is not plotted, using the first 30 seconds only is an acceptable approximation of the initial rate.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the rate of an enzyme-catalysed reaction fall as the reaction proceeds?","options":{"A":"Substrate concentration decreases, so fewer complexes form","B":"The enzyme is used up in forming the products.","C":"The activation energy increases over the course of the reaction.","D":"Product molecules always denature the enzyme on contact."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-11","stem":"In an enzyme experiment, a buffer solution is added in order to:","options":{"A":"Increase the reaction rate by supplying extra substrate.","B":"Prevent the enzyme from binding to its substrate too quickly.","C":"Raise the temperature to the enzyme's optimum value.","D":"Keep pH constant so it is not an uncontrolled variable"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"A buffer resists pH change, holding pH constant so any observed effect can be attributed to the independent variable. Controlled variables in a typical enzyme practical: temperature (water bath), pH (buffer), enzyme and substrate concentration and volume, and time. Repeats allow a mean and reveal anomalies; identifying the independent, dependent and controlled variables correctly is what makes the investigation valid.","reforge":{"stem":"A student investigates the effect of temperature on amylase. Which is the dependent variable?","options":{"A":"The temperature of each water bath used.","B":"The time taken for starch to be digested","C":"The concentration of the amylase solution.","D":"The volume of buffer added to each tube."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-12","stem":"Glycolysis differs from the Krebs cycle in that glycolysis:","options":{"A":"Occurs in the cytoplasm and needs no oxygen","B":"Produces the majority of a cell's ATP directly.","C":"Takes place in the mitochondrial matrix of the cell.","D":"Requires oxygen to be present in order to proceed."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-RESPIRATION-PHOTOSYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Aerobic respiration stages: glycolysis (cytoplasm, anaerobic, glucose → 2 pyruvate, net 2 ATP + 2 reduced NAD); link reaction (matrix, pyruvate → acetyl CoA + CO₂ + reduced NAD); Krebs cycle (matrix, per turn: 2 CO₂, 3 reduced NAD, 1 reduced FAD, 1 ATP); oxidative phosphorylation (inner membrane, electron transport chain and chemiosmosis, ~26-28 ATP, oxygen as final electron acceptor). Most ATP comes from the last stage, not from glycolysis.","reforge":{"stem":"Oxygen is described as the final electron acceptor in aerobic respiration. Without it:","options":{"A":"Glycolysis alone would stop immediately in the cytoplasm.","B":"The Krebs cycle would continue at an increased rate.","C":"The chain backs up and reduced NAD is not reoxidised","D":"ATP would still be produced normally by chemiosmosis."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.7"},{"id":"BIO-N4-13","stem":"In anaerobic respiration in muscle, pyruvate is converted to lactate in order to:","options":{"A":"Produce a large additional quantity of ATP directly.","B":"Regenerate oxidised NAD so glycolysis can continue","C":"Provide carbon dioxide for transport in the blood.","D":"Store energy in a form that can be respired in the mitochondria."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-RESPIRATION-PHOTOSYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Without oxygen the electron transport chain stops, so reduced NAD cannot be reoxidised there. Reducing pyruvate to lactate (animals) or to ethanol and CO₂ (yeast and plants) oxidises reduced NAD back to NAD, allowing glycolysis and its small ATP yield to continue. No further ATP is made in the conversion itself. Lactate is later transported to the liver and oxidised back to pyruvate or converted to glycogen, which requires oxygen — the oxygen debt.","reforge":{"stem":"Yeast respiring anaerobically produces ethanol and carbon dioxide, whereas muscle produces lactate only. Both pathways share the function of:","options":{"A":"Supplying pyruvate to the Krebs cycle in the matrix.","B":"Generating 38 molecules of ATP per glucose molecule.","C":"Producing carbon dioxide for use in photosynthesis.","D":"Oxidising reduced NAD so glycolysis can continue"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.7"},{"id":"BIO-N4-14","stem":"A respiratory quotient (RQ) of 1.0 indicates that the substrate being respired is:","options":{"A":"Protein, respired during prolonged starvation.","B":"Lipid, which requires more oxygen per carbon atom.","C":"Carbohydrate, respired aerobically","D":"A mixture of lipid and protein in equal proportion."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-RESPIRATION-PHOTOSYNTHESIS","scaffold":"RQ = CO₂ produced ÷ O₂ consumed. Carbohydrate gives 1.0 (C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O). Lipid gives about 0.7 — lipids are more reduced, so more oxygen is needed per carbon released. Protein is around 0.9. An RQ above 1.0 indicates some anaerobic respiration is occurring, since CO₂ is produced without corresponding oxygen uptake. RQ is measured with a respirometer, using soda lime to absorb CO₂.","reforge":{"stem":"In a respirometer, potassium hydroxide solution is included in order to:","options":{"A":"Absorb carbon dioxide, so movement shows oxygen use","B":"Maintain a constant temperature inside the apparatus.","C":"Prevent the organisms from respiring anaerobically.","D":"Supply oxygen to the respiring organisms in the tube."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-15","stem":"In the light-independent reaction, the enzyme rubisco catalyses the combination of carbon dioxide with:","options":{"A":"Triose phosphate, forming glucose directly.","B":"Chlorophyll, releasing electrons into the transport chain.","C":"Reduced NADP, forming ATP for the light-dependent stage.","D":"Ribulose bisphosphate, forming two molecules of GP"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-RESPIRATION-PHOTOSYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Calvin cycle: CO₂ + RuBP (5C) → 2 × GP (3C), catalysed by rubisco; GP is reduced to triose phosphate (TP) using reduced NADP and ATP from the light-dependent stage; most TP regenerates RuBP using further ATP, and a small proportion leaves to make glucose, amino acids and lipids. Six turns fix six CO₂ and yield one hexose. Limiting either CO₂ or light stops the cycle — a standard data question.","reforge":{"stem":"If a plant is suddenly deprived of carbon dioxide, the immediate effect on the Calvin cycle is that:","options":{"A":"Triose phosphate accumulates while GP concentration falls.","B":"RuBP accumulates while GP concentration falls","C":"Both RuBP and GP concentrations rise sharply together.","D":"The light-dependent reaction stops before the Calvin cycle."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.6"},{"id":"BIO-N4-16","stem":"Photolysis of water during the light-dependent reaction supplies:","options":{"A":"Electrons to replace those lost from photosystem II","B":"Carbon dioxide for fixation by rubisco in the stroma.","C":"ATP directly to the light-independent reaction.","D":"Glucose for use in respiration by the plant cell."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-RESPIRATION-PHOTOSYNTHESIS","scaffold":"Light excites electrons in photosystem II, which pass along the electron transport chain in the thylakoid membrane; the energy released pumps protons into the thylakoid space, and their return through ATP synthase makes ATP (chemiosmosis). Photolysis (2H₂O → 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ + O₂) replaces the lost electrons, supplies protons for reducing NADP, and releases oxygen as a by-product — the origin of atmospheric oxygen.","reforge":{"stem":"The oxygen released by a photosynthesising plant originates from:","options":{"A":"Carbon dioxide absorbed through the stomata.","B":"Glucose broken down in the mitochondria.","C":"Water molecules split during photolysis","D":"Reduced NADP produced in the stroma."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-17","stem":"Negative feedback maintains homeostasis by ensuring that a change in a factor triggers a response that:","options":{"A":"Amplifies the original change until a threshold is reached.","B":"Reverses the change, returning the factor to its set point","C":"Has no effect until the change becomes dangerously large.","D":"Permanently resets the body's normal set point to a new value."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Negative feedback loop: receptor detects deviation from the set point → coordinator (often the hypothalamus or an endocrine gland) processes it → effector produces a response that opposes the change → the factor returns towards normal and the stimulus is removed. Separate mechanisms for raising and lowering a factor allow far more sensitive control. Positive feedback amplifies a change instead — as in oxytocin during labour or the depolarisation of a neurone.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these is an example of positive rather than negative feedback?","options":{"A":"Insulin secretion lowering a raised blood glucose concentration.","B":"Sweating in response to a rise in core body temperature.","C":"Vasoconstriction reducing heat loss when the body cools.","D":"Oxytocin release increasing uterine contractions during labour"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.1"},{"id":"BIO-N4-18","stem":"Insulin lowers blood glucose concentration principally by:","options":{"A":"Stimulating the conversion of glycogen into glucose in the liver.","B":"Reducing the rate of respiration in all body cells.","C":"Increasing glucose uptake by cells and its conversion to glycogen","D":"Preventing glucose from being absorbed in the small intestine."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Beta cells of the islets of Langerhans secrete insulin when glucose is high: more glucose transporters move into cell membranes, uptake and respiration increase, and glycogenesis (glucose → glycogen) occurs in liver and muscle. Alpha cells secrete glucagon when glucose is low: glycogenolysis (glycogen → glucose) and gluconeogenesis (glucose from non-carbohydrate sources) in the liver. Adrenaline also raises glucose. Note the similar names carefully — glycogenesis stores, glycogenolysis releases.","reforge":{"stem":"Type 1 diabetes differs from type 2 in that in type 1 the:","options":{"A":"Beta cells are destroyed, so little insulin is made","B":"Body produces excessive quantities of insulin.","C":"Liver is unable to store any glycogen at all.","D":"Cells become less responsive to normal insulin levels."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-19","stem":"The loop of Henle enables the production of concentrated urine by:","options":{"A":"Filtering large plasma proteins out of the blood in the glomerulus.","B":"Actively reabsorbing all glucose from the filtrate.","C":"Secreting additional water into the distal convoluted tubule.","D":"Creating a high solute concentration in the medulla"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"The countercurrent multiplier: the ascending limb actively pumps Na⁺ and Cl⁻ into the medullary tissue fluid and is impermeable to water; this lowers the water potential of the medulla, so water leaves the descending limb by osmosis, concentrating the filtrate. The gradient built up means that as filtrate passes down the collecting duct, water can leave by osmosis. A longer loop gives a steeper gradient — desert mammals have notably long loops.","reforge":{"stem":"ADH increases water reabsorption by making the collecting duct walls:","options":{"A":"Impermeable to water, so filtrate stays dilute.","B":"More permeable to water, by inserting aquaporins","C":"Able to actively transport water against its gradient.","D":"Permeable to sodium ions but not to water."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-20","stem":"Ultrafiltration at the glomerulus separates substances mainly according to:","options":{"A":"Molecular size, with proteins and blood cells retained","B":"Their solubility in the lipid bilayer of the capillary wall.","C":"Their electrical charge, with all negative ions retained.","D":"Whether they can be actively transported by carrier proteins."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"High hydrostatic pressure in the glomerulus (the efferent arteriole is narrower than the afferent) forces small molecules — water, glucose, urea, ions — through the capillary fenestrations, basement membrane and podocyte filtration slits. The basement membrane is the actual filter, retaining plasma proteins and blood cells. Glucose is then entirely reabsorbed at the proximal convoluted tubule by co-transport, which is why glucose in urine suggests diabetes.","reforge":{"stem":"Protein in the urine suggests damage to which structure?","options":{"A":"The loop of Henle in the kidney medulla.","B":"The collecting duct's response to ADH.","C":"The basement membrane of the glomerulus","D":"The proximal convoluted tubule microvilli."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.2"},{"id":"BIO-N4-21","stem":"During strenuous exercise, core body temperature is prevented from rising excessively by:","options":{"A":"Vasoconstriction of skin arterioles and shivering.","B":"Vasodilation of skin arterioles and increased sweating","C":"Raising the metabolic rate of the liver further.","D":"Erection of body hairs to trap an insulating air layer."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Cooling responses: vasodilation of skin arterioles brings more blood near the surface so more heat is radiated; sweating uses the high latent heat of vaporisation of water; hairs lie flat; metabolic rate falls. Warming responses: vasoconstriction, shivering (involuntary muscle contraction releasing heat), hairs erected to trap air, and increased metabolic rate. The hypothalamus coordinates both, using thermoreceptors in the skin and in the hypothalamus itself.","reforge":{"stem":"Sweating cools the body effectively because water:","options":{"A":"Has a very low specific heat capacity.","B":"Reduces the metabolic rate of the skin cells.","C":"Conducts heat away rapidly through the skin.","D":"Requires much energy to evaporate"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-22","stem":"A resting neurone maintains its resting potential of about −70 mV mainly through the action of:","options":{"A":"Voltage-gated sodium channels opening along the axon.","B":"The myelin sheath insulating the axon membrane.","C":"The sodium-potassium pump, 3 Na⁺ out per 2 K⁺ in","D":"Diffusion of chloride ions into the axon cytoplasm."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"At rest, the sodium-potassium pump uses ATP to move 3 Na⁺ out for every 2 K⁺ in; the membrane is more permeable to K⁺, which leaks back out, leaving the inside negative relative to the outside. Depolarisation: a stimulus opens voltage-gated Na⁺ channels, Na⁺ floods in and the potential rises to about +40 mV. Repolarisation: Na⁺ channels close, K⁺ channels open and K⁺ leaves. Hyperpolarisation then follows before the resting potential is restored.","reforge":{"stem":"Saltatory conduction increases the speed of an impulse along a myelinated axon because depolarisation:","options":{"A":"Occurs only at the nodes of Ranvier, so the impulse jumps","B":"Happens simultaneously along the entire length of the axon.","C":"Requires no movement of sodium ions at all.","D":"Is prevented by the refractory period at each node."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.3"},{"id":"BIO-N4-23","stem":"The refractory period following an action potential is important because it:","options":{"A":"Increases the amplitude of each successive action potential.","B":"Allows the impulse to travel in both directions equally.","C":"Prevents the sodium-potassium pump from using any ATP.","D":"Ensures impulses travel one way and limits their frequency"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"During the refractory period the voltage-gated sodium channels are inactivated, so no new action potential can be generated in that region. Consequences: the impulse cannot travel backwards, so it is unidirectional; action potentials are discrete and separated; and there is an upper limit to firing frequency. Because action potentials are all-or-nothing and constant in size, a stronger stimulus is encoded as a higher frequency, not a larger amplitude.","reforge":{"stem":"Action potentials are described as all-or-nothing. A stronger stimulus is therefore represented by:","options":{"A":"A larger amplitude for each individual action potential.","B":"A higher frequency of action potentials","C":"A slower conduction speed along the axon.","D":"A longer refractory period between impulses."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.3"},{"id":"BIO-N4-24","stem":"At a cholinergic synapse, the arrival of an action potential at the presynaptic membrane causes:","options":{"A":"Calcium ions to enter, so vesicles fuse and release ACh","B":"Acetylcholinesterase to be released into the cleft.","C":"Sodium channels on the presynaptic membrane to close.","D":"Direct electrical flow of ions across the synaptic cleft."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Sequence: action potential depolarises the presynaptic membrane → voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open → Ca²⁺ influx causes vesicles of acetylcholine to fuse with the membrane and release by exocytosis → ACh diffuses across the cleft and binds receptors on the postsynaptic membrane → Na⁺ channels open, and if threshold is reached an action potential is generated. Acetylcholinesterase then hydrolyses ACh, so the response stops and the products are recycled.","reforge":{"stem":"A drug that inhibits acetylcholinesterase would cause the postsynaptic neurone to:","options":{"A":"Stop responding to any further stimulation.","B":"Release its own neurotransmitter into the cleft.","C":"Continue to be stimulated, as ACh remains","D":"Become permanently hyperpolarised and inactive."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.3"},{"id":"BIO-N4-25","stem":"Which structure in a muscle fibre contains the calcium ions released during contraction?","options":{"A":"The mitochondrion in the sarcoplasm.","B":"The sarcoplasmic reticulum","C":"The Z line at the end of each sarcomere.","D":"The myosin head within the thick filament."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GBIO-HOMEOSTASIS","scaffold":"Sliding filament theory: an impulse travels down T-tubules → the sarcoplasmic reticulum releases Ca²⁺ → Ca²⁺ binds troponin, moving tropomyosin and exposing binding sites on actin → myosin heads attach forming cross-bridges → the power stroke pulls actin inward, using ADP release → ATP binds and detaches the head, and hydrolysis re-cocks it. The sarcomere shortens: the I band and H zone narrow while the A band stays the same length.","reforge":{"stem":"During contraction, the sarcomere shortens. Which observation is correct?","options":{"A":"The Z lines move further apart from each other.","B":"The A band shortens while the I band lengthens.","C":"Both the actin and myosin filaments become shorter.","D":"The A band stays the same while the H zone narrows"},"correct":"D"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"},{"id":"BIO-N4-26","stem":"ATP is described as the universal energy currency of cells because it:","options":{"A":"Stores far more energy per molecule than glucose does.","B":"Can be stored in large quantities for long periods.","C":"Releases a usable amount of energy in one hydrolysis","D":"Is produced only within the mitochondria of eukaryotic cells."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GBIO-RESPIRATION-PHOTOSYNTHESIS","scaffold":"ATP = adenine + ribose + three phosphates. Hydrolysis of the terminal phosphate by ATP hydrolase releases about 30.6 kJ mol⁻¹ — a small, immediately usable packet, unlike glucose which releases far more in one go and needs many steps. ATP is not a long-term store: it is made and used continuously, resynthesised from ADP + Pi by ATP synthase. Its roles include active transport, muscle contraction, synthesis reactions and phosphorylating other molecules to make them more reactive.","reforge":{"stem":"ATP is a poor long-term energy store compared with glycogen or lipid because ATP is:","options":{"A":"Unstable and continuously recycled, not stored","B":"Produced only during anaerobic respiration in muscle.","C":"Unable to be hydrolysed once it has been synthesised.","D":"Insoluble in the cytoplasm of most animal cells."},"correct":"A"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-5.7"},{"id":"BIO-N4-27","stem":"In a colorimeter experiment measuring enzyme activity, a calibration curve is produced in order to:","options":{"A":"Increase the rate of the enzyme-catalysed reaction.","B":"Ensure the colorimeter remains at a constant temperature.","C":"Remove any anomalous results from the data set.","D":"Convert absorbance readings into a concentration value"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GBIO-INVESTIGATION","scaffold":"A colorimeter measures how much light of a chosen wavelength a solution absorbs. Known standard concentrations are measured first and plotted as a calibration curve; unknown samples are then read off against it. Set the colorimeter to zero with a blank (distilled water or the reaction mixture without substrate), and choose the filter of the colour most strongly absorbed. This gives quantitative, continuous data rather than a subjective colour judgement.","reforge":{"stem":"Using a colorimeter rather than judging colour by eye improves an investigation mainly because the data are:","options":{"A":"Collected more quickly from each sample tube.","B":"Objective and quantitative, reducing bias","C":"Guaranteed to be free from all experimental error.","D":"Unaffected by the concentration of the solution."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"BIO-ENZ","specGranularity":"bank","specPointId":"ocr-a-bio-2.4"}]}});
